6.1
[Critical] A rigged STREAM entity can drain other entities’ principal from the shared
VanaPoolTreasury
File(s): /vanaPoolStaking/VanaPoolStakingImplementation.sol, /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol
Description: Every VanaPool entity keeps its funds in one shared VanaPoolTreasury contract, and each entity is a share based pool:
activeRewardPool records the VANA it owns and totalShares records the claims against it. Entities are created by a maintainer on behalf
of a third party operator, who becomes the entity owner and controls that entity’s reward scheduling. A staker who exits before their
bonding period matures is returned their principal and forfeits accrued rewards.
Shares are not minted in a single division. The stake(...) function of the VanaPoolStaking contract first asks the VanaPoolEntity contract
for a rate, computed by the vanaToEntityShare(...) function as (totalShares * 1e18) / activeRewardPool, and then applies that rate
to the deposit:
1
uint256 vanaToShare = vanaPoolEntity.vanaToEntityShare(entityId);
2
// @audit-issue The rate returned above is already floored, so the depositor
3
// receives floor(r) / r of the shares paid for, not a single wei less.
4
uint256 sharesIssued = (vanaToShare * stakeAmount) / 1e18;
Because the truncation lands on the rate rather than on the result, a depositor receives floor(r) / r of the shares they paid for. For an
ordinary entity the rate is around 1e18 and the loss is invisible. When the true rate sits just below an integer m, the depositor receives only
(m - 1) / m of their shares, which is as little as half at m = 2. Their wei still enters activeRewardPool, so the shortfall silently accrues to
whoever already holds shares.
An entity owner can move their own entity into that regime using only owner level calls. The registration stake written by the registerEntity
Stake(...) function never sets rewardEligibilityTimestamp, so it is unbonded from birth and the owner can withdraw all but a chosen
dust share count. The owner then calls the distributeRewards(...) function, which is owner callable on a STREAM entity, to vest VANA
into activeRewardPool without minting any shares. Leaving S0 shares against a parked amount P fixes the rate at S0 * 1e18 / P, so the
owner selects m directly. This is not reachable in APY mode, where the drip computed by the calculateYield(...) function is proportional
to activeRewardPool and a dust pool therefore drips dust.
The second half of the problem is that a stake made while the position is already inside its bonding period is booked at raw msg.value
rather than at the value of the shares actually minted. The branch is selected by reading rewardEligibilityTimestamp as it was stored
before the call, so a brand new address takes the eligible branch on its first ever deposit and is booked at value; it becomes bonded only
as that same call returns. Raw wei treatment therefore applies from the second deposit onwards, and a position that is fully exited keeps
its rewardEligibilityTimestamp, so the bond survives indefinitely once armed:
1
} else {
2
// @audit-issue The cost basis grows by raw wei, while the shares bought
3
// with those wei are under-issued by the floored rate.
4
stakerEntity.costBasis += stakeAmount;
5
// ...
6
}
An early exit then refunds that raw cost basis, while the entity’s books are reduced by only the smaller share value. Both happen inside
the _unstake(...) function:
1
} else {
2
// @audit-issue The treasury pays out the raw cost basis.
3
vanaToReturn = proportionalCostBasis;
4
// ...
5
}
6
7
// ...
8
9
// @audit The entity gives up only shareValue, which is less than the refund.
10
vanaPoolEntity.updateEntityPool(entityId, shareAmount, shareValue, false);
The attacker therefore dilutes themselves inside their own entity and is then refunded pre-dilution. Because all entities share one treasury,
the difference is paid out of other entities’ deposits. One cycle at m = 2 against 1000e18 wei of parked capital proceeds as follows:
1. The entity is created with the 1e18 wei registration stake, minting 1e18 shares against an activeRewardPool of 1e18 wei. The rate is
1e18 and the entity behaves normally;
2. The owner unstakes all but 2000 shares. The registration position is unbonded, so this succeeds immediately and leaves 2000
shares against 2000 wei;
6

NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
3. The owner calls the distributeRewards(...) function with 1000e18 wei of their own VANA and a duration of 0, and the next call to
the processRewards(...) function moves it into activeRewardPool. The entity now holds 1000e18 + 2000 wei against 2000 shares,
so the true rate is 1.999999999999999996, the vanaToEntityShare(...) function returns 1, and one share is worth 5e17 wei;
4. A second address controlled by the attacker makes a seed deposit of 1e18 wei. Its stored rewardEligibilityTimestamp is still 0
when the branch is chosen, so this first deposit takes the eligible branch, is booked at the value of the shares minted, and opens
no gap. Its only purpose is to leave the position bonded, which the same call does before returning;
5. The same address then deposits 1414e18 wei. This time the branch test reads a rewardEligibilityTimestamp in the future, so
costBasis grows by the raw 1414e18 wei to a total of 1414.5e18 wei, while the 1415 shares held are worth only about 1000.65e18
wei;
6. That address exits before the bond matures. The treasury refunds the raw 1414.5e18 wei while the entity’s books give up only
the 1000.65e18 wei of share value. Treasury cash is barely moved across the deposits and the refund, but the roughly 414e18 wei
difference stays in activeRewardPool, backed by no cash and owned entirely by the owner’s 2000 shares;
7. The owner’s dust position is therefore worth about 1414.35e18 wei instead of the 1000e18 wei that was parked. When the owner
finally unstakes it, the treasury pays out that full amount, and the roughly 413.85e18 wei of net gain is real cash belonging to the
stakers of other entities;
The impacts are the following:
−Any honest depositor into such an entity loses up to 50% of their deposit to the entity owner’s dust share position, on a single
ordinary stake(...) call. The shareAmountMin parameter is the only defence and is opt-in;
−The shortfall is taken from other entities’ stakers. No honest counterparty is required, since both the dust position and the
depositing address belong to the attacker. Each cycle deposits b and refunds b, so treasury cash is flat while the entity’s books
inflate; the cash leaves only when the owner finally unstakes the dust position, and it is paid out of other entities’ principal;
−The total is not bounded by the attacker’s capital. Repeating the cycle walks the rate from m down to 1, and the ceiling per entity
is park * (m - 1). Since m is chosen freely by how far the registration stake is drained, bounded only by S0 <= 1e18, there is no
meaningful limit in code. The practical cap is the honest TVL present in the treasury;
