CB-097,FR-230,A malicious entity owner can capture or withhold the rewards the splitter has already paid to their entity’s stakers,Nethermind__NM0451-1052-FINAL_Vana_Staking.pdf,Nethermind,vana,2026-09-29,yes,yes,2026-09,High,"NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.2
[High] A malicious entity owner can capture or withhold the rewards the splitter
has already paid to their entity’s stakers
File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol, /rewardSplitter/RewardSplitterImplementation.sol
Description: The RewardSplitter contract splits a governance-funded VANA budget across VanaPool entities in proportion to the stake-
seconds each entity accrued since the previous round, so an entity’s share is earned by the stake its delegators committed over time.
The splitter pays that share by calling the addRewards(...)
function on the VanaPoolEntity contract, which only credits the entity’s
lockedRewardPool. For a STREAM entity, that balance reaches delegators only once a vesting schedule is installed by the distributeRewards(
...) function, which is callable by the entity owner (or a maintainer) and accepts an unconstrained duration, including 0 for an instant
release.
Rewards are therefore not credited to the stakers who earned them at the moment the splitter pays; they wait under the owner’s control.
Whenever they do vest, the processRewards(...) function skims the owner’s commission at the rate in force at that moment, and MAX_-
COMMISSION is 100%.
1
// @audit-issue The rate applied is the one current when the amount vests, which
2
// need not be the rate that was advertised when the amount was funded.
3
uint256 commission = (toDistribute * entity.commissionRate) / MAX_COMMISSION;
4
uint256 delegatorReward = toDistribute - commission;
5
6
entity.lockedRewardPool -= toDistribute;
7
entity.activeRewardPool += delegatorReward; // to delegators via share price
8
entity.accruedCommission += commission; // operator's cut, claimable
The updateEntityCommission(...) function settles pending rewards before applying a new rate, which is intended to make rate changes
forward-only. That guard does not protect funds which have not been scheduled yet: the owner raises the rate first, so the settlement has
nothing to settle and the whole balance later vests under the new rate.
1. The splitter pays the entity its share X, earned by the delegators’ stake-seconds. X sits in lockedRewardPool, vesting nothing;
2. The owner calls updateEntityCommission(entityId, MAX_COMMISSION). Its settlement covers nothing, and the rate becomes 100%;
3. The owner calls distributeRewards(entityId, X, block.timestamp, 0) to install an instant entry. This function also settles before
installing that entry, so again nothing vests at the old rate;
4. In any later block, the owner calls claimCommission(entityId). It settles first, so X vests in full, commission equals X, delegatorReward
is 0, and X is transferred from the treasury to the owner;
Steps 2 and 3 can share a single transaction. Step 4 has to be in a later block only because processRewards(...) returns early when no
time has elapsed since the last settlement.
Once that first transaction has landed, the outcome cannot be prevented, even though MAINTAINER_ROLE can call the same functions the
owner used. Settlement is permissionless, so any later interaction with the entity completes the capture without the owner acting again. A
maintainer lowering the rate does not help, because the updateEntityCommission(...) function settles before applying the new rate, so
their own transaction vests the balance at 100% and pays the owner. Pausing does not help either: settlement is whenNotPaused, so the
rate cannot be changed at all while the contract is paused, and the first settlement after unpausing still applies 100%.
The owner stakes nothing, risks nothing and serves no bonding period, since claimCommission(...) pays accruedCommission directly
rather than through share value. The entire budget the splitter attributed to the delegators is taken: activeRewardPool never grows, so
their share price does not move, and totalDistributedRewards records nothing as distributed. The owner can then restore the advertised
rate, so the commissionRate a delegator reads afterwards is not the rate their rewards were taken at.
Entities are onboarded by a
maintainer, but an entity owner is otherwise an independent operator competing for delegations.
Two weaker variants of the same root cause are available even with a bounded commission rate. The owner can stake a large amount,
close the entity to others with the updateEntityStakingBlocked(...) function, then release X instantly so that their snapshot position
captures most of it, and unstake once the bonding period passes; an early exit still returns the principal, so the downside is roughly zero.
Alternatively the owner can simply never call the distributeRewards(...) function, leaving X parked indefinitely while the entity keeps
accruing stake-seconds and drawing further budget in every later round. Delegators cannot force the release, and unstaking abandons
the balance, because it belongs to the entity rather than to the stakers whose stake earned it and will accrue to whoever holds shares
when it is finally released. This last variant is the one a maintainer can undo, since the distributeRewards(...) function is callable by
MAINTAINER_ROLE as well, so governance can schedule a parked balance without the owner’s cooperation.
Recommendation(s): Consider revisiting the assumption that an entity owner may decide the terms on which externally funded rewards
reach delegators. Rewards the splitter has already attributed to an entity’s stakers should not be exposed to parameters the owner can
change afterwards, and the owner should not be able to choose the moment at which those rewards land relative to their own position
or their own commission rate. Consider also whether an owner should be able to leave such a balance unreleased indefinitely, and what
recourse delegators or governance should have when that happens.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client:
Fixed in commit 2a6c350 with 3 parts:
−Dedicated, owner-proof reward track. Splitter rewards now go through addStakerRewards(entityId, payCommission, duration)
( REWARD_SPLITTER_ROLE-gated), into a separate stakerLockedRewardPool + stakerRewardSchedule kept out of lockedRewardPool.
No owner function — distributeRewards, switchToStreamModel, sweep, or the APY/STREAM drip — can reach it, so the owner
9

