A staker who holds a matured position in one entity and a bonded position in another, and who moves the second into the first puts their
previously safe rewards back at risk. If they exit before the newly imposed bond elapses, the unstaking logic takes the bonding branch,
returns only the cost basis, and routes the difference back into the entity’s locked pool for other stakers and the entity owner’s commission.
Recommendation(s): Consider mirroring the logic that the staking path already performs, so that a matured destination position has
its unrealised gain converted into cost basis before any new bonding deadline is applied to it. Consider also reviewing whether the
documented promise that no forfeiture occurs during redelegation holds for every combination of source and destination maturity.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client:
Pushed — c583cc7. Here’s the auditor summary:
[Medium] Redelegating into a matured position makes earned rewards forfeitable — Fixed (commit c583cc7)
Root cause confirmed: stake into a matured position crystallizes the earned gain into cost basis before re-bonding (costBasis = newTotal-
Value), so a later bonding-period exit still returns it. redelegate’s destination side did not — it added only the moved principal (to.costBasis
+= movedCostBasis) and wrote a new future eligibility over the merged position. A staker holding a matured position in A and a bonded
position in B who redelegated B→A had A re-bonded with its gain not crystallized; exiting A before the new bond took the bonding branch
and forfeited that gain — breaking the documented ""full value travels, no forfeiture.""
Fix: on the destination, before applying the new bond, crystallize to’s gain when it is matured (mirrors stake’s eligible branch):
if (existingRemaining == 0 to.shares > 0 existingValue > to.costBasis) to.vestedRewards += existingValue - to.costBasis; to.costBasis =
existingValue;
redelegate is now a proper superset of the manual path — it preserves B’s rewards (which the manual path forfeits on B’s bonded exit)
