6
? (entity.totalShares * 1e18) / entity.activeRewardPool
7
: 1e18;
8
}
This state can be reached when the last holder exits. The VANA returned to them is computed through two successive floor divisions,
so whenever the share supply does not divide the pool exactly, a remainder is left behind while the share count reaches zero. With an
activeRewardPool of 4e18 against a totalShares of 3e18, the final exit returns 3999999999999999999 and strands 1 wei.
From that point on the entity is unusable. Every call to the staking function computes zero shares issued and reverts, and every redel-
egation into the entity reverts on its slippage check, so the share supply can never become non-zero again. The activeRewardPool is
decreased through paths that now revert, so the entity remains listed as active while its lockedRewardPool, including every payout the
reward splitter routes to it, is unrecoverable.
Recommendation(s): Consider making both conversion functions agree on a single definition of the empty entity, keyed on the share sup-
ply, so that an entity holding residual VANA with no shares outstanding still quotes a bootstrap price. Additionally, consider sweeping any
residual activeRewardPool back into the locked pool at the moment the share supply reaches zero, so that the dust does not accumulate
against an entity with no holders.
