_addStaker(ownerAddress);
Every reward in the unstaking logic compares the current time against rewardEligibilityTimestamp using a >= comparison, so the default
value of 0 is read as a deadline that elapsed. The entity owner is therefore eligible for rewards on their collateral, they may withdraw it at
any moment, receive the full share value including all accrued rewards, and forfeit nothing, while every ordinary staker in the same entity
who deposited at the same time must serve the full bonding period or forfeit.
Collateral was intended to be locked and the evidence comes from the contract itself, which declares a dedicated error for this case, but it
is never used.
1
// @audit-issue This error is declared but is never used anywhere in the codebase.
2
error CannotRemoveRegistrationStake();
Beyond the unequal treatment between an entity creator and a regular staker, removing the collateral leaves an entity that is still listed
as active and eligible to receive reward splitter payouts, with no operator stake behind it. It also produces the empty entity state that the
reward stream capture issue depends on.
Recommendation(s): Consider assigning a bonding deadline to the registration position when it is created, so the operator is subject
to the same forfeiture rules as the delegators they attract. Consider additionally enforcing the already declared restriction so that the
collateral cannot be withdrawn while the entity remains active.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed. Bonding period is attached to the registration stake. Also, the owner cannot unstake and leave the stake
in the pool less than minRegistrationStake.
Update from the client (addendum): the seed is bonded in registerEntityStake (0d83fc5) and the non-removable floor is recorded per
registrant in VanaPoolStakingStorageV3 (32f757f), so neither unstake, unstakeVana, redelegate-out nor an ownership transfer can take
totalShares below it.
