6.10
[Low] A one wei deposit can capture an entire elapsed reward stream
File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol
Description: Entities may vest rewards under a stream model, in which a scheduled amount is released linearly over a time period. The
_vestStream(...) function from the VanaPoolEntity contract computes how much of the active schedule has matured since its last update
and advances a checkpoint so the same interval is never paid twice.
When an entity has no shares, there is nobody to receive a distribution, so the function returns early. Deliberately, and as documented in
the comment, it does so without advancing the checkpoint, in order to preserve the elapsed interval for whoever stakes next.
1
function _vestStream(
2
RewardSchedule storage schedule,
3
uint256 totalShares
4
) internal returns (uint256 toVest) {
5
// @audit-issue The checkpoint is not advanced, so the whole elapsed interval
6
// is preserved and paid to whoever holds shares next.
7
if (schedule.scheduledValue == 0 || totalShares == 0) {
8
return 0;
9
}
10
// ...
11
}
The preserved value is not reserved for the stakers it was intended for; it is paid to whatever share supply exists at the next settlement.
Because the entity is empty, the share price falls back to 1:1, so a deposit of 1 wei mints one share and makes the depositor the sole
owner of the entity. Settling the rewards then moves the entire preserved amount into a pool backed by that single share.
Crucially, the guard that protects the APY model does not apply here. The staking function settles rewards before it mints shares, which
for an APY entity credits any backlog to the preexisting holders. For a stream entity that settlement hits the early return above and credits
nothing, so the new depositor’s share is minted first and the stream vests to them afterwards.
Recommendation(s): Consider advancing the checkpoint when an entity has no shares outstanding, so that no vesting accrues to an
empty pool, and returning the elapsed portion to the entity’s residue instead. If preserving the interval is a deliberate product requirement,
consider gating the preserved amount so that it can only be released to a share supply that existed while it accrued.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed (d8e459d). _vestStream now advances the watermark (and promotes ended entries) even when total-
Shares == 0; the elapsed portion vests to nobody and stays in lockedRewardPool as re-schedulable residue rather than being preserved
for the next depositor. Reproduced your PoC: after 50 days empty, a 1 wei deposit was worth 50 ether + 1 wei; after the fix, dust, with the
50 ether retained and fully re-schedulable by the owner. Tests: StreamEmptyPoolCapture.t.sol, VestStream.t.sol.
