NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.11
[Low] Switching reward models leaves a stale stream schedule installed
File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol
Description: An entity vests rewards either through a continuously compounding APY drip or through a linear stream, and a maintainer
may move an entity between the two models in either direction using the updateEntityRewardModel(...) function from the VanaPoolEntity
contract.
The two models keep separate clocks: the APY drip advances lastUpdateTimestamp, while the stream advances its own
checkpoint inside the schedule.
The dedicated function that migrates an entity into the stream model clears any previously installed schedule, and its comment explains
that this is to prevent a stale entry from surviving.
1
processRewards(entityId);
2
entity.rewardModel = RewardModel.STREAM;
3
// @audit This sibling function clears the schedule before installing a new one.
4
delete entity.rewardSchedule;
The general model switch performs no equivalent clearing. It settles the outgoing model and then flips the flag, leaving the schedule and
its checkpoint untouched.
1
processRewards(entityId);
2
// @audit-issue The reward schedule and its checkpoint survive the switch to APY.
3
entity.rewardModel = model;
While the entity sits in APY mode, the drip consumes the same lockedRewardPool that still escrows the untouched stream, and the escrow
invariant is only ever checked when a new distribution is scheduled. Switching back reactivates a schedule whose checkpoint predates
the entire APY phase and whose window may have elapsed in the meantime, so the next settlement releases the remaining escrow as a
single lump rather than linearly.
A proof of concept installs a 100 ether stream over 100 days, an intended rate of 1 ether per day, switches to APY after one day, runs the
drip for 200 days, and switches back. The next settlement releases 65.510030697081739698 ether in a single block and empties the locked
pool entirely. Any shortfall between what the resurrected schedule claims and what remains in the locked pool is silently absorbed by a
clamp that runs after the checkpoint has already moved, so the difference can never be vested afterwards.
Recommendation(s): Consider making the general model switch symmetric with the dedicated one, so that leaving the stream model
clears or anchors the schedule and its checkpoint. Consider also re-asserting the escrow invariant whenever an entity reenters the stream
model, so that a schedule can never be reactivated against a pool that no longer backs it.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed (d898734).
updateEntityRewardModel was removed entirely; the only switch is the one-way switch-
ToStreamModel, which deletes any existing schedule before installing a new one.
Update from Nethermind: fixed at commit. The function has been removed. The switchToStreamModel function should be used instead.
18

