NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.12
[Low] The VANA denominated unstake disables the minimum output check
File(s): /vanaPoolStaking/VanaPoolStakingImplementation.sol
Description: The VanaPoolStaking contract offers two ways to withdraw. The share denominated unstake(...) function forwards a
caller-supplied minimum VANA amount, which the internal logic enforces against the amount actually returned. The VANA denominated
unstakeVana(...) function converts the requested amount into shares and then delegates to the same internal logic.
It passes a hard-coded zero in place of that minimum, so the guard its sibling offers is disabled on this path.
1
// @audit-issue The minimum VANA output is hard coded to zero on this path.
2
_unstake(staker, entityId, shareAmount, 0, true);
The only protection available to the caller bounds the shares burned rather than the VANA received, and it is applied after a silent clamp,
so a request larger than the position is quietly reduced to the whole position instead of reverting.
1
if (shareAmount > stakerEntity.shares) {
2
// @audit-issue An oversized request is silently reduced instead of reverting.
3
shareAmount = stakerEntity.shares;
4
}
5
6
if (shareAmountMax > 0 && shareAmount > shareAmountMax) {
7
revert InvalidSlippage();
8
}
A caller who passes the documented skip value for the share bound therefore executes with no protection on either side. The realistic
exposure is modest, because the share price is monotonically non-decreasing and both conversions occur in the same transaction, but
the asymmetry between the two withdrawal functions is unlikely to be intentional.
Recommendation(s): Consider offering the same value denominated protection on both withdrawal paths, so that a caller specifying an
amount in VANA can bound what they receive. Consider also reverting rather than silently clamping when the requested amount exceeds
the position, so that callers are not served a materially different operation from the one they requested.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed (77ab6a5), per your recommendation. unstakeVana takes a fourth parameter, vanaAmountMin, forwarded
to _unstake exactly as unstake() does, and reverts with InvalidAmount on a request larger than the position instead of silently clamping.
unstakeVana is not in the mainnet ABI, so the signature change affects no integrator. We deliberately did not make vanaAmount itself
the implicit floor: shares are floored from the request and valued by a floored rate, so the payout lands 1–2 wei under the request on any
non-1:1 price (measured 365/365 sampled requests), and an exact floor reverted essentially every call; the docstring directs callers to a
floor with dust tolerance. Tests: UnstakeVanaMinOut.t.sol.
19

