File(s): /vanaPoolStaking/VanaPoolStakingImplementation.sol
Description: The protocol advertises a single bonding period, and the updateBondingPeriod(...) function from the VanaPoolStaking
contract validates that the configured value never exceeds a hard ceiling.
1
function updateBondingPeriod(uint256 newBondingPeriod) external onlyRole(MAINTAINER_ROLE) {
2
// @audit The ceiling is validated here, on the CONFIGURED value only.
3
if (newBondingPeriod > MAX_BONDING_PERIOD) {
4
revert InvalidBondingPeriod();
5
}
6
bondingPeriod = newBondingPeriod;
7
// ...
8
}
The deadline actually written to a position is not that value but a quotient, and nothing validates the result against either the configured
period or the ceiling.
1
// @audit-issue Neither branch bounds the result by bondingPeriod or MAX_BONDING_PERIOD.
2
stakerEntity.rewardEligibilityTimestamp =
3
currentTimestamp + (stakeAmount * bondingPeriod) / newTotalValue;
4
// ...
5
uint256 weightedTime =
6
(oldValue * remainingTime + stakeAmount * bondingPeriod) / newTotalValue;
This produces two behaviours that the advertised period does not describe. First, newTotalValue is derived through successive floor
divisions, so for a very small deposit it lands below stakeAmount and the quotient overshoots. Measured against a share price of 1.1188
and a configured period of 432000 seconds, the smallest deposit that mints a single share receives a bond of 864000 seconds, exactly 2
times the configured value; the factor is 1 + 1/n for n shares minted and has decayed to nothing by a deposit of 1e6 wei. With the period
set to MAX_BONDING_PERIOD, the same deposit yields 63072000 seconds against a stated ceiling of 31536000. No economically meaningful
deposit is affected, but the ceiling is not the invariant it appears to be.
Second, in the opposite direction, a top-up made part-way through a bond discards most of the time already served on the entire position,
because the blend is recomputed over the combined value. With a period of 30 days, a position ten days into its bond has 1728000 seconds
remaining; adding ten times the position returns the remaining time to 2513454 seconds, close to a full restart. This is the intended anti-
wash behaviour rather than a defect, but it is not documented for users and it is the same formula that, in the matured-anchor case reported
separately, collapses the bond toward zero instead.
Recommendation(s): Consider bounding the computed deadline so that a position can never be bonded for longer than the configured
period, and re-asserting the ceiling on the result rather than only on the configuration. Consider also documenting that adding to a
position recomputes the deadline for the whole position, since both the overshoot and the near-restart surprise users who reason about
the advertised period alone.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed (0c30045). The stake bonding-branch blend and the redelegate destination blend are capped at bond-
ingPeriod, mirroring the existing partial-unstake and redelegate-source clamps. Reproduced 28.8-day and 29.0-day deadlines written
against a 7-day period after a governance reduction; both now 7 days. Upper bound only, so the value-weighted behaviour accepted under
the separate Medium is unchanged. Tests: BondingDeadlineBound.t.sol.
Update from Nethermind: the if (stakerEntity.rewardEligibilityTimestamp <= currentTimestamp) branch doesn’t bound
rewardEligibilityTimestamp to the bondingPeriod. The same capping mechanism should be applied to this branch as well.
Update from the client: Confirmed and fixed (602e9e8). stake()’s eligible branch now caps stakeAmount · bondingPeriod / newTotal-
Value at bondingPeriod, consistent with the other three deadline writes. The overshoot arises because newTotalValue is the minted shares’
floored value: at a high price per share a deposit worth 1.9 shares mints one, giving a ratio of 1.9. Test test_eligibleBranchDeadlineIs-
CappedAtBondingPeriod: such a deposit into a 5e11 wei/share pool now receives exactly bondingPeriod (before: 1.9×).
