4
vanaPoolEntity.calculateYield(simulatedActiveRewardPool, entityInfo.maxAPY, timeElapsed);
5
if (toDistribute > entityInfo.lockedRewardPool) {
6
toDistribute = entityInfo.lockedRewardPool;
7
}
8
simulatedActiveRewardPool += toDistribute;
Actual settlement branches on the entity’s reward model and credits only the delegator portion of the amount released. For an entity using
the stream model the view invents a drip that will never occur, while ignoring the linear vesting that will, so the quote is wrong in both
directions. For any entity with a non-zero commission the simulated pool is overstated by the commission share. The structure of the data
the view reads makes a correct answer impossible, since the entity information struct carries neither the reward model nor the commission
rate.
There is no direct impact on funds, as the function does not modify state and the returned share count is clamped to the staker’s actual
holdings. The consequence falls on integrators, whose withdrawals either revert against a slippage bound or return less than quoted. The
view additionally reports the treasury balance as available headroom for a single entity, when that balance is shared across all entities.
Recommendation(s): Consider having the view consult the entity’s reward model, schedule and commission rate so that it reproduces
what settlement will actually credit. A more robust alternative is to expose a preview of reward settlement on the VanaPoolEntity contract
and have the view call it, so that the quoting and executing paths cannot drift apart as the reward logic evolves.
Status: Fixed
