NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.15
[Low] Unclamped decrement of distributed rewards blocks early unstaking
File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol
Description: When a staker exits before their bonding period elapses, the rewards they forfeit are returned to the entity so they can be
redistributed. The returnForfeitedRewards(...) function from the VanaPoolEntity contract performs that return and also decrements the
entity’s cumulative totalDistributedRewards counter, since the forfeited amount is no longer considered distributed.
1
entity.lockedRewardPool += amount;
2
// @audit-issue This subtraction has no lower bound and reverts on underflow.
3
entity.totalDistributedRewards -= amount;
The sibling function that moves the same amount between entities during a redelegation explicitly guards the identical subtraction, and its
comment states precisely why.
1
// @audit The author clamps the same counter here, but not in the function above.
2
uint256 debit = amount < from.totalDistributedRewards ? amount : from.totalDistributedRewards;
3
from.totalDistributedRewards -= debit;
The counter only ever accumulates the delegator portion of each distribution, while share price appreciation caused by floor rounding
raises the value of existing positions without being credited to it. The difference between the sum of forfeitable gains and the counter is
therefore thin, and a small residual is enough to invert the comparison. Because the minimum stake amount is not enforced, a deposit of
2 wei is sufficient to inject such a residual.
Once the counter is short, the affected staker’s early exit reverts with an arithmetic panic inside the reward return, and their principal is
inaccessible. The DOS is temporary rather than permanent: once the bonding period elapses, the matured branch of the unstaking logic
runs, nothing is forfeited, and the reward return is never reached, so the position can always be withdrawn eventually.
Recommendation(s): Consider applying the same clamp that the redelegation path already uses, so that the counter returns zero instead
of reverting. Consider also reviewing whether totalDistributedRewards should track rounding-induced appreciation at all, so that the two
quantities being compared cannot drift apart.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Update from the client: Fixed (f3c72e3). returnForfeitedRewards now clamps totalDistributedRewards to zero
instead of underflowing, matching the redelegation path.
