File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/VanaPoolEntityImplementation.sol
Description: The _vestStream(...) function from the VanaPoolEntityImplementation contract is used to compute how much a schedule
has vested. The function converts the block.timestamp value to a uint32 and stores it in the schedule.lastUpdate variable. Since this
is a Unix timestamp expressed in seconds, the maximum value that a uint32 can hold corresponds to approximately 136 years. Given
that the Unix epoch started in 1970 and the current year is 2026, 56 years have already passed. This means the contract will behave as
expected for about 80 more years until the uint32 cast silently overflows and breaks the reward vesting logic.
1
function _vestStream(RewardSchedule storage schedule, uint256 totalShares) internal returns (uint256 toVest) {
2
// ...
3
// @audit Casting block.timestamp to uint32 will overflow in approximately 80 years.
4
schedule.lastUpdate = uint32(block.timestamp);
5
}
6
Recommendation(s): Consider updating the timestamp storage type to a larger data type to prevent potential overflow in the future.
