NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.20
[Info] The max amount quoted by getMaxUnstakeAmount(...) can be rejected by
unstakeVana(...) after the minimum-stake-on-exit rule
File(s): contracts/vanaStaking/vanaPoolStaking/VanaPoolStakingImplementation.sol
Description: The getMaxUnstakeAmount(...) view is what an interface calls to populate a ""max"" button: it returns both maxVana (the most
VANA a staker can take out) and maxShares (the same ceiling expressed in shares). For a reward eligible position it quotes the full value
of the staker’s shares.
1
uint256 userShareValue = (stakerEntity.shares * shareToVana) / 1e18;
2
// ...
3
} else {
4
// @audit Reward eligible: the quote is the full value of ALL the shares.
5
userWithdrawable = userShareValue;
6
}
A caller that then passes maxVana to unstakeVana(...) has the amount converted back to a share count with vanaToShares(...), which
floors. Because maxVana was itself produced by a flooring multiplication, the round trip lands a few share units below the staker’s full
holding, so the burn leaves a tiny remainder rather than clearing the position.
1
shareAmount = vanaPoolEntity.vanaToShares(entityId, vanaAmount);
The minimum-stake-on-exit rule added in the re-review then rejects that remainder, because it is worth far less than minStakeAmount.
1
uint256 remainingShares = stakerEntity.shares - shareAmount;
2
// @audit-issue The dust left by the maxVana round trip is below minStakeAmount,
3
// so the call quoted as the maximum reverts.
4
if (remainingShares > 0 && (remainingShares * shareToVana) / 1e18 < minStakeAmount) {
5
revert InsufficientStakeAmount();
6
}
The result is a mismatch between what the view promises and what the withdrawal accepts: for a matured position whose share price
is not a whole number (which is any position that has accrued rewards), calling unstakeVana(...) with the quoted maxVana reverts with
InsufficientStakeAmount. There is no impact on funds, and the staker can still exit in full by using the share denominated path with the
quoted maxShares, or by lowering the requested amount so the remainder clears the minimum. The consequence is limited to the honesty
of the maxVana figure on the VANA denominated withdrawal path: an integrator that wires the ""max"" button to unstakeVana(maxVana) will
see it revert. This is the same class of view versus execution mismatch as the original maximum-unstake finding, reintroduced on the
VANA path by the new exit rule.
Recommendation(s): Consider making the VANA denominated maximum consistent with what the withdrawal will accept, so that the
quoted value either clears the position entirely or leaves at least the minimum, or documenting that the share denominated maxShares is
the value to use for a full exit.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: unstakeVana now serves a request that would strand a sub-minimum remainder as the full exit it amounts to,
provided shareAmountMax admits the whole position (the maxShares returned alongside maxVana; 0 is not consent) and the registration
floor allows it, so unstakeVana(maxVana, maxShares, . . . ) clears a matured position even against a slightly stale quote, while any other
request keeps the existing exit rule and nothing is burned beyond the caller’s bound.
26

