NM-1052 - VANA STAKING - SECURITY REVIEW
6.21
[Info] Unstaking cost grows linearly with the number of entities ever created
File(s): /vanaPoolStaking/VanaPoolStakingImplementation.sol
Description: The VanaPoolStaking contract keeps an active and an inactive staker list. Adding a staker to the active list is a constant-time
operation performed whenever a position is opened. Removing one, which happens on every single unstake, first determines whether the
staker still holds a position anywhere, by scanning every entity identifier that has ever been issued.
1
function _removeStaker(address staker) internal {
2
uint256 entitiesCount = vanaPoolEntity.entitiesCount();
3
bool hasStake = false;
4
// @audit-issue This scans every entity ever created, on every unstake.
5
for (uint256 i = 1; i <= entitiesCount; i++) {
6
if (_stakers[staker].entities[i].shares > 0) {
7
hasStake = true;
8
break;
9
}
10
}
11
// ...
12
}
Each iteration reads a distinct cold storage slot, and the loop bound only ever increases, because the entity counter is never decremented
and entity removal is commented out. The scan also runs on partial withdrawals where the position clearly survives, and the early exit
only helps stakers whose lowest-numbered position is found quickly, so a staker whose only position is a recently created entity always
pays the full scan.
The result is that the cost of leaving the protocol grows without bound as the protocol grows, and can never be reduced, while the cost
of entering remains constant. Entity creation is restricted to a maintainer, so the counts at which withdrawal would become genuinely
impossible are not attacker-reachable; the realistic impact is a permanent and steadily increasing burden on the one operation that must
always remain available to users.
Recommendation(s): Consider tracking each staker’s non-empty positions directly, for example with a set that is updated as positions
are opened and closed, so that list membership can be determined without scanning the entire entity space. Consider also avoiding the
check entirely on partial withdrawals where the position demonstrably survives.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Partially addressed (2b5849b). _removeStaker’s scan now runs only on a full exit; a partial withdrawal leaves
the position alive, so the staker is known to be active and the scan is skipped (behaviour unchanged, pinned by test_partialUnstake-
KeepsStakerActive_fullExitDeactivates). Replacing the full-exit scan with a per-staker position count needs a storage slot and a migration
for existing stakers; acknowledged and left as a follow-up.
6.22
