6.25
[Best Practices] The calculateYield(...) interface declares its first two param-
eters in the wrong order
File(s): /vanaPoolEntity/interfaces/IVanaPoolEntity.sol
Description: The VanaPoolEntity contract exposes a pure helper that integrators can call to reproduce the protocol’s yield calculation.
The interface and the implementation disagree about what the first two arguments mean.
1
// @audit-issue The interface declares apy first and principal second.
2
function calculateYield(
3
uint256 apy,
4
uint256 principal,
5
uint256 time
6
) external pure returns (uint256);
The implementation takes them in the opposite order, as principal then apy. All three parameters are uint256, so the selector is identical,
the override compiles silently, and the single in-repository caller passes them positionally in the implementation’s order and is therefore
correct. Any integrator coding against the published interface will transpose the two values and receive a meaningless result, with no
revert to signal it.
Recommendation(s): Consider renaming the interface parameters so that they match the implementation, and reviewing the other
interface declarations for the same kind of drift.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client: Fixed (2b5849b). Interface renamed to (principal, apy, time) to match the implementation. Per your recommenda-
tion we scanned all three interfaces for the same drift and aligned the two other mismatches (IVanaPoolStaking.unstake’s share parameter
was named amount; IVanaPoolEntity.updateVanaPool’s parameter name).
6.26
[Best Practices] The buy and burn sink can be cleared while a reserve is still
outstanding
File(s): /rewardSplitter/RewardSplitterImplementation.sol
Description: The RewardSplitter contract sets aside a configurable fraction of each distributed budget in pendingBurn, which is later
flushed to the configured sink. The updateBuyAndBurn(...) function requires a non-zero sink only when the rate itself is non-zero.
1
// @audit-issue The sink may be set to the zero address whenever the rate is
2
// @audit-issue zero, including while pendingBurn is still non-zero.
3
if (newBurnRate > 0 && newBuyAndBurnAddress == address(0)) {
4
revert InvalidAddress();
5
}
Disabling the feature by passing a zero rate and a zero sink is therefore accepted even when a reserve has already accrued. In that state
the flush reverts on its own zero address check, while both the distribution and the withdrawal paths subtract pendingBurn from the balance
they are allowed to touch, so the reserve is simultaneously unspendable and unwithdrawable. It is recoverable by re-configuring a sink, so
this is a configuration trap rather than a loss.
Recommendation(s): Consider requiring a non-zero sink whenever a reserve is outstanding, regardless of the rate being set, so that the
feature cannot be switched off in a way that strands funds already set aside.
Status: Fixed
Update from the client:
Removed the configurable sink entirely instead of guarding it. The cut now burns directly to address(0):
buyAndBurnAddress and updateBuyAndBurn(rate, addr) (with the flagged zero-address check) removed. Added BURN_ADDRESS = address(0);
updateBurnRate(rate) sets only the rate; executeBurn() sends pendingBurn to BURN_ADDRESS unconditionally (permissionless). The trap
is gone: no sink to misconfigure, and pendingBurn is always flushable via executeBurn() regardless of rate — so it can’t be stranded.
