CB-119,FR-252,"Caller-supplied fee and positive slippage parameters let any user strip protocol fees, partner fees and positive slippage capture from swaps",Nethermind__NM1048-FINAL_INFRARED.pdf,Nethermind,infrared,2026-09-09,yes,yes,2026-09,High,"Issues
6.1
[High] Caller-supplied fee and positive slippage parameters let any user strip
protocol fees, partner fees and positive slippage capture from swaps
File(s): src/Router.sol#L95-L111, src/Router.sol#L276-L286, src/Router.sol#L357-L392
Description: Users obtain a route from the Infrared API ( /v1/quote, open to anonymous callers) and then call /v1/build, which returns
calldata for the swap(...), swapMulti(...), swapPermit2(...) or swapMultiPermit2(...) functions of the Router contract. The Router
holds all economic logic: it deducts the protocol fee from the input, pays an optional partner fee, and, when passPositiveSlippageToUser
is false, keeps any output above outputQuote as positive slippage. According to the documentation, the protocol fee is ""resolved from
your subscription tier (and per-account overrides). Never caller-supplied."", and ""the platform protocol fee and positive-slippage routing are
derived server-side from the caller’s subscription tier"". The fee a user pays is therefore the product that paid tiers buy.
However, the Router cannot tell whether the parameters it receives are the ones the backend produced. Every economic field is plain
calldata:
1
struct SwapParams {
2
// ...
3
// @audit Caller-controlled ceiling above which positive slippage is captured.
4
uint256 outputQuote;
5
// ...
6
// @audit-issue The whole fee and slippage policy is unauthenticated calldata.
7
uint16 protocolFeeBps;
8
uint16 partnerFeeBps;
9
address partnerRecipient;
10
bool partnerFeeOnOutput;
11
bool passPositiveSlippageToUser;
12
// ...
13
}
The _validateSwapCommon(...) function (and _validateMultiSwap(...) for multi-swaps) only enforces upper bounds and a non-zero
partner recipient. There is no lower bound, no on-chain fee configuration and no backend signature over the parameters:
1
// @audit-issue Only upper bounds are enforced; a zero fee is always accepted.
2
if (p.protocolFeeBps > MAX_PROTOCOL_FEE_BPS) revert ProtocolFeeExceedsCap(p.protocolFeeBps);
3
if (p.partnerFeeBps > MAX_PARTNER_FEE_BPS) revert PartnerFeeExceedsCap(p.partnerFeeBps);
4
if (p.partnerFeeBps > 0 && p.partnerRecipient == address(0)) revert InvalidPartnerRecipient();
The _executeSwap(...) function (and _processPulledInputs(...) with _settleOutputs(...) for multi-swaps) then applies whatever the
caller supplied:
1
function _executeSwap(...) internal returns (uint256 amountOut) {
2
// @audit-issue A zero `protocolFeeBps` or `partnerFeeBps` removes the fee entirely.
3
uint256 protocolFee = (pulled * params.protocolFeeBps) / 10_000;
4
uint256 inputPartnerFee =
5
params.partnerFeeOnOutput ? 0 : (pulled * params.partnerFeeBps) / 10_000;
6
// ...
7
uint256 positiveSlippage;
8
// @audit-issue Setting `passPositiveSlippageToUser` to true, or inflating
9
// `outputQuote`, disables the capture.
10
if (!params.passPositiveSlippageToUser && amountOut > params.outputQuote) {
11
positiveSlippage = amountOut - params.outputQuote;
12
amountOut = params.outputQuote;
13
}
14
// ...
15
}
Consequently, a user can build the calldata with a free or low tier key (keys are self-service via POST /v1/keys), decode the SwapParams,
set protocolFeeBps = 0, partnerFeeBps = 0 and passPositiveSlippageToUser = true, lower inputAmount to the post-fee amount the
Weiroll program already consumes, and submit it. They receive the same route that paying subscribers get (multi-hop, split and multi-
input/multi-output paths) without paying any fee, while the protocol and any integrator receive nothing. The Swap event reports zero fees,
so off-chain accounting cannot flag it, and the permissionless Router cannot enforce the tier afterwards. The Router is already deployed
on Ethereum, Base and Arbitrum One.
Note that on-chain checks alone cannot fully close this gap, because the asset being sold is the route and the route is handed to the
user in executable form. The ExecutionProxy is ""stateless and permissionless"" by design, so anyone can send their tokens to it and call
executePath(...) directly with the returned weirollCommands and weirollState, skipping the Router entirely. Even if the ExecutionProxy
were restricted to the Router and the Router verified its parameters, the Weiroll program is still disclosed in the calldata, so a user can
6

NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
deploy their own copy of the VM and run the identical route. On-chain enforcement is therefore a guardrail that raises the effort, not a
complete fix, and the residual exposure has to be consciously accepted.
Recommendation(s): Consider deciding explicitly how the tier-based fee model is enforced, since the contracts do not provide the
guarantee the documentation describes. The options carry different trade-offs:
−On-chain guardrails. Consider having the backend sign the economic parameters (fee fields, outputQuote, passPositiveSlippageToUser,
the hash of the Weiroll program, the taker and an expiry) with a rotatable signer the Router verifies, or moving the fee policy on-
chain through an owner-managed protocol fee and registered partner configurations that callers can reference but not lower. This
stops the trivial calldata edit but not direct execution through the ExecutionProxy or a redeployed VM, so it should come with explicit
acceptance of that residual risk;
−Off-chain gating. Consider serving /v1/build to paying subscribers only, so fees are collected through the subscription and the
on-chain fee fields become far less critical, at the cost of free tier reach;
−Relayed execution for the free tier. Consider not returning calldata to free users and submitting the transaction on their behalf so
the route never leaves Infrared’s infrastructure. The trade-off is that Infrared pays the gas and needs a way to recover it from the
user, for example from the swap output, plus the relayer infrastructure;
Whichever option is chosen, consider documenting the resulting trust model for integrators and correcting the public documentation so it
no longer states that the protocol fee is never caller-supplied.
Status: Fixed at commit 09cb18b
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 09cb18b (contracts, PR #18).
Every swap entry point (swap,
swapMulti,
swapPermit2,
swapMultiPermit2) now takes a backend-signed EIP712 Authorization (nonce, expiry, signature) over the complete parameter struct,
the taker ( msg.sender), the chain and the Router address (domain InfraredRouter); the fee fields, outputQuote(s),
passPositiveSlippageToUser, the partner fields and the keccak of the Weiroll program and state are all inside the signed hash. The
_verifyAuthorization runs before validation and before any pull, so a call whose parameters differ from the signed values reverts
InvalidAuthorization; unsigned calls are rejected outright (there is no unsigned fallback). Each authorization is single-use
(consumedAuthorizations, AuthorizationAlreadyUsed), expiry is capped at MAX_AUTHORIZATION_TTL (1 hour), and the signer is owner-
managed via setSigner(newSigner,
grace) with a bounded overlap (MAX_SIGNER_GRACE, 1 hour) plus a fail-closed revokeSigner()
[...excerpt ends]"
CB-120,FR-253,A partner can reenter into the proxy while it has funds,Nethermind__NM1048-FINAL_INFRARED.pdf,Nethermind,infrared,2026-09-09,yes,yes,2026-09,Low,"NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
6.2
[Low] A partner can reenter into the proxy while it has funds
File(s): src/Router.sol, src/ExecutionProxy.sol
Description: The swapMulti(...) function lets a user swap several input tokens in one atomic transaction. Third party integrators can be
credited a referral fee on those swaps by being set as the partnerRecipient, and the fee is paid out of each input token as that input is
processed. Partners are external integrators rather than a protocol defined trusted role: the Router pays them a commission, but it does
not entrust them with user funds.
Inputs are prepared one at a time inside a loop in the _processPulledInputs(...) function. For each token the partner fee is paid out
first, and the remainder is then staged to the executor:
1
for (uint256 i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
2
address token = params.inputTokens[i];
3
// ...
4
if (inputPartnerFee > 0) {
5
// @audit-issue The partner receives control here, before this slot is
6
// staged and after every earlier slot already has been.
7
_transferOut(token, params.partnerRecipient, inputPartnerFee);
8
}
9
// ...
10
if (token == NATIVE_ETH_SENTINEL) {
11
nativeForwardAmount = forward;
12
effectiveInputToken = NATIVE_ETH_SENTINEL;
13
} else {
14
IERC20(token).safeTransfer(exec, forward);
15
}
16
}
For a native ETH slot the payout is performed by _transferOut(...) using a low level call with no gas limit, so the partnerRecipient
contract can reenter:
1
if (token == NATIVE_ETH_SENTINEL) {
2
// @audit-issue Full gas is forwarded to a caller-supplied address.
3
(bool ok, ) = to.call{value: amount}("""");
4
if (!ok) revert ETHTransferFailed();
5
}
The native slot is not required to be the first one. The _validateMultiSwap(...) function only scans for the first native entry in order to
reconcile msg.value, so an input array such as [USDC, USDT, NATIVE] is a valid configuration. On the third iteration the partner can execute
arbitrary code at a moment when the first two slots’ tokens are already sitting in the executor.
The nonReentrant modifier on swapMulti(...) does not protect those funds. It guards the Router’s own state, but the tokens are held by
the Executor contract at this point, and the executePath(...) function on that contract has no restrictions and no reentrancy guard of its
own. From the callback the partner simply calls executePath(...) with a program that transfers the tokens to themselves. The executor’s
documentation justifies having no guard on the premise that ""the Router enforces the nonReentrant boundary"", which does not hold for
this window.
This means a malicious partner can take a portion of a victim’s input tokens mid transaction, assuming the resulting shortfall stays within
the victim’s slippage tolerance. _settleOutputs(...) still clears every outputMins[j], the swap completes normally, and the emitted Swap
event reports a successful trade, so the theft leaves no visible trace. Extraction per swap is bounded by the difference between the realized
output and outputMin, and it is repeatable across every user the partner routes.
Recommendation(s): Consider adding an ""onlyRouter"" modifier on the Executor contract. Restricting executePath(...) to the Router
contract which implements nonReentrant already, prevents this attack.
Status: Fixed at commit 6400441
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 6400441 (same change as the access-control finding).
With executePath Router-only, a
partnerRecipient that gains control during the native-ETH fee payout can no longer run a program against the inputs already staged
on the executor; every execution sits behind the Router’s nonReentrant boundary. Reproduced with a ReentrantPartner mock reentering
from receive() during swapMulti with inputs [tokenA, NATIVE]: with the gate removed the theft succeeds and the swap still settles; with
it the reentry reverts NotRouter, the staged balance is intact and the swap completes.
8

NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
6.3
[Low] The _execute(...) function misparses revert data that is not an Error(string)
File(s): src/weiroll/VM.sol
Description: The Weiroll virtual machine runs each command in a program and, when a sub call fails, unwraps the failure reason so it can
be surfaced in a typed ExecutionFailed error. The unwrapping assumes the sub call reverted with a string error, whose encoding places
the string’s length at byte offset 36 of the returndata.
The only guard applied before that assumption is a non-empty check:
1
if (!success) {
2
// @audit-issue Only emptiness is checked, not the shape of the payload.
3
if (outdata.length > 0) {
4
assembly {
5
outdata := add(outdata, 68)
6
}
7
}
8
revert ExecutionFailed({
9
// @audit-issue The failing command index is hardcoded although `i` is in scope.
10
command_index: 0,
11
target: address(uint160(uint256(command))),
12
message: outdata.length > 0 ? string(outdata) : ""Unknown""
13
});
14
}
Advancing the pointer by 68 bytes moves the length word to what was byte offset 36 of the returndata. For an Error(string) payload
that offset holds the string length. For a custom error carrying two words it holds the error’s second argument, which is then treated as
a byte length and copied when the message is encoded. The protocol’s own InsufficientOutputAmount(uint256 amountOut, uint256
minAmountOut) error has that shape, so a slippage failure inside a Weiroll path causes minAmountOut to be read as a memory length.
Payloads shorter than 68 bytes are read past their allocation instead, returning zero and collapsing the message to ""Unknown"".
The impact is twofold. A transaction failing inside a Weiroll path consumes more gas than intended and reverting corrupts the data.
Recommendation(s): Consider verifying both that the payload is long enough and that its selector corresponds to Error(string) before
advancing the pointer, and bubbling the raw returndata unchanged otherwise. The loop index should also be reported instead of the
hardcoded zero.
Status: Fixed at commit 030d0d4
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 030d0d4. The VM now classifies revert data before touching it: only a provably well-formed
Error(string) (selector, 68-byte header, offset 0x20, length within bounds) is unwrapped into ExecutionFailed, empty data maps to
""Unknown"", and every other payload (custom errors, Panic, malformed data) is re-raised byte-for-byte. The reported index is the position
of the failing command’s first word in commands[]. Covered by a test suite built from Nethermind’s PoC scenarios and gas harness with the
assertions inverted to the fixed behaviour (raw returndata checked byte-for-byte under a fixed gas cap, including the 1e18 second-word
case, the three-word leak, and consumption across allowances); 10 of the 11 fail on the old VM and all pass on the fix. ExecutionProxy
salt namespace rotated to .v4 for redeploy.
9

