CB-121,FR-254,The _execute(...) function misparses revert data that is not an Error(string),Nethermind__NM1048-FINAL_INFRARED.pdf,Nethermind,infrared,2026-09-09,yes,yes,2026-09,Low,"NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
6.3
[Low] The _execute(...) function misparses revert data that is not an Error(string)
File(s): src/weiroll/VM.sol
Description: The Weiroll virtual machine runs each command in a program and, when a sub call fails, unwraps the failure reason so it can
be surfaced in a typed ExecutionFailed error. The unwrapping assumes the sub call reverted with a string error, whose encoding places
the string’s length at byte offset 36 of the returndata.
The only guard applied before that assumption is a non-empty check:
1
if (!success) {
2
// @audit-issue Only emptiness is checked, not the shape of the payload.
3
if (outdata.length > 0) {
4
assembly {
5
outdata := add(outdata, 68)
6
}
7
}
8
revert ExecutionFailed({
9
// @audit-issue The failing command index is hardcoded although `i` is in scope.
10
command_index: 0,
11
target: address(uint160(uint256(command))),
12
message: outdata.length > 0 ? string(outdata) : ""Unknown""
13
});
14
}
Advancing the pointer by 68 bytes moves the length word to what was byte offset 36 of the returndata. For an Error(string) payload
that offset holds the string length. For a custom error carrying two words it holds the error’s second argument, which is then treated as
a byte length and copied when the message is encoded. The protocol’s own InsufficientOutputAmount(uint256 amountOut, uint256
minAmountOut) error has that shape, so a slippage failure inside a Weiroll path causes minAmountOut to be read as a memory length.
Payloads shorter than 68 bytes are read past their allocation instead, returning zero and collapsing the message to ""Unknown"".
The impact is twofold. A transaction failing inside a Weiroll path consumes more gas than intended and reverting corrupts the data.
Recommendation(s): Consider verifying both that the payload is long enough and that its selector corresponds to Error(string) before
advancing the pointer, and bubbling the raw returndata unchanged otherwise. The loop index should also be reported instead of the
hardcoded zero.
Status: Fixed at commit 030d0d4
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 030d0d4. The VM now classifies revert data before touching it: only a provably well-formed
Error(string) (selector, 68-byte header, offset 0x20, length within bounds) is unwrapped into ExecutionFailed, empty data maps to
""Unknown"", and every other payload (custom errors, Panic, malformed data) is re-raised byte-for-byte. The reported index is the position
of the failing command’s first word in commands[]. Covered by a test suite built from Nethermind’s PoC scenarios and gas harness with the
assertions inverted to the fixed behaviour (raw returndata checked byte-for-byte under a fixed gas cap, including the 1e18 second-word
case, the three-word leak, and consumption across allowances); 10 of the 11 fail on the old VM and all pass on the fix. ExecutionProxy
salt namespace rotated to .v4 for redeploy.
9

NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
6.4
[Low] The output partner fee can make a validated range of outputMin values
revert
File(s): src/Router.sol
Description: A swap carries two output limits. The outputQuote value is the amount the backend quoted, and the outputMin value is the
absolute minimum amount the user must be credited. Two separate features then reduce the amount the executor delivered before that
minimum is checked. When passPositiveSlippageToUser is false, anything above outputQuote is retained by the protocol and the user
is credited exactly outputQuote. When partnerFeeOnOutput is true, the partner fee is denominated in the output token and deducted from
what the user is credited.
The validation accepts any outputMin up to and including outputQuote:
1
// @audit Equality is permitted, so `outputMin == outputQuote` is a valid input.
2
if (p.outputMin > p.outputQuote) revert InvalidSlippageBounds();
The execution path then caps the amount, deducts the fee from the capped value, and only afterwards compares against the minimum:
1
uint256 positiveSlippage;
2
if (!params.passPositiveSlippageToUser && amountOut > params.outputQuote) {
3
positiveSlippage = amountOut - params.outputQuote;
4
// @audit The surplus is retained here, so the fee below is charged on
5
// `outputQuote` rather than on the amount the executor delivered.
6
amountOut = params.outputQuote;
7
}
8
9
uint256 outputPartnerFee;
10
if (params.partnerFeeOnOutput && params.partnerFeeBps > 0) {
11
outputPartnerFee = (amountOut * params.partnerFeeBps) / 10_000;
12
// @audit-issue The fee is subtracted from the capped amount.
13
amountOut -= outputPartnerFee;
14
_transferOut(params.outputToken, params.partnerRecipient, outputPartnerFee);
15
}
16
17
// @audit-issue The minimum is checked last, against the twice-reduced amount.
18
if (amountOut < params.outputMin) {
19
revert SlippageExceeded(params.outputToken, amountOut, params.outputMin);
20
}
Because amountOut is capped at outputQuote once the cap engages, the most the user can ever be credited is outputQuote minus the
partner fee. Validation, however, allows outputMin all the way up to outputQuote. Every outputMin above the reduced amount therefore
reverts no matter how well the trade executed.
This affects any swap configured with partnerFeeOnOutput set to true, a non-zero partnerFeeBps, and passPositiveSlippageToUser set
to false. With an outputQuote of 1000e18 and a partnerFeeBps of 200, the most the user can be credited is 980e18, so every outputMin
above 980e18 is unreachable. A one percent tolerance places outputMin at 990e18, already inside that range, so the swap cannot succeed.
This means that there are cases in which a parameter combination the validation logic accepts as valid, can never settle in reality, and the
user pays gas for a transaction that could not have succeeded.
Recommendation(s): Consider enforcing the outputMin floor against the amount before the output-side partner fee is deducted, or
alternatively tightening the validator so that it rejects an outputMin that the configured partnerFeeBps makes unreachable. Whichever
approach is taken should be mirrored in _settleOutputs(...).
Status: Fixed at commit 8c64a74
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 8c64a74 by tightening the validator (second recommended option). outputQuote stays the
gross ceiling and outputMin the net floor, which is how the backend already sizes them; _validateSwapCommon and _validateMultiSwap
now compute the reachable ceiling outputQuote - fee(outputQuote) (capture on, output-side fee) with the same flooring as settlement
and revert OutputMinUnreachable(outputMin, maxReachable) before pulling any input. Pass-through or no output fee leaves behaviour
unchanged. The previous test encoded the flagged case as a post-execution SlippageExceeded; it is replaced by validation, ceiling,
short-swap and pass-through tests plus a multi-swap mirror, and the short-payment fuzz oracle now covers the ceiling.
10

