NM-1048 — INFRARED ROUTER — SECURITY REVIEW
6.6
[Info] The executePath(...) function has no access control while it custodies
in-flight user funds
File(s): src/ExecutionProxy.sol, src/Router.sol
Description: The ExecutionProxy contract is the Weiroll virtual machine that the Router contract delegates trade execution to. During a
swap the Router transfers the user’s input tokens to the executor and then asks it to run the trade program. The executor is documented
as intentionally stateless and permissionless, holding no approvals and no economic logic.
The executePath(...) function that runs the program is callable by anyone:
1
// @audit-issue There is no caller restriction, no owner and no reentrancy guard.
2
function executePath(bytes32[] calldata commands, bytes[] calldata state)
3
external
4
payable
5
override
6
{
7
_execute(commands, state);
8
}
The Weiroll virtual machine supports calls to arbitrary targets with arbitrary calldata and arbitrary native value, so executePath(...) is a
general-purpose ""make this contract do anything"" primitive available to every address. The contract has no owner and no sweep function,
and the receive() and fallback() functions are both payable.
At the same time, the Router hands custody of user funds to that contract inside _forwardToExecutor(...):
1
IERC20(token).safeTransfer(exec, amount);
2
IExecutor(exec).executePath(commands, state);
This means that any token or native balance the executor holds outside an in-flight swap can be taken by anyone using a two command
program that reads balanceOf(...) and then calls transfer(...).
The Router forwards an amount to a program whose amounts are encoded off-chain before the transaction runs, and it never checks that
the executor’s balance returned to zero afterwards. The only post condition it enforces is that the output amount is >= outputMin.
Recommendation(s): Consider storing the Router address in the executor at construction time and restricting executePath(...) so that
only the Router can invoke it. This ensures that the only entrypoint into the virtual machine is the Router contract.
Status: Fixed at commit 6400441
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 6400441. ExecutionProxy now takes the Router in its constructor (immutable ROUTER) and
executePath reverts NotRouter(caller) for any other sender, so only the Router can drive the VM and any balance parked on the executor
is unreachable to third parties. receive()/ fallback() stay payable for Router-forwarded ETH and WETH unwraps. Deploy script and
verification updated for the constructor argument. Covered by tests for a third-party caller, a third-party sweep of parked funds, and the
same program succeeding when the Router drives it. Same commit resolves the partner-reentrancy finding.
12

