File(s): src/weiroll-helpers/UniswapV4SwapHelpers.sol
Description: The UniswapV4SwapHelpers contract is a Weiroll command target that performs a single-pool Uniswap V4 exact-input swap
through the Universal Router and returns the realized output amount. It is deployed once per chain as a CREATE3 singleton shared by
every user of the protocol, the swapExactInSingle(...) function is public payable with no access control, the receiver of the proceeds
is a caller-supplied parameter, and the receive() function accepts native ETH from anybody.
After the Universal Router call returns, the function determines the swap’s output by reading its own total balance rather than the difference
across the call:
1
UNIVERSAL_ROUTER.execute{value: msg.value}(commands, inputs, deadline);
2
3
if (currencyOut == address(0)) {
4
// @audit-issue This is an absolute balance read, not a pre/post delta.
5
amountOut = address(this).balance;
6
} else {
7
// @audit-issue This is an absolute balance read, not a pre/post delta.
8
amountOut = IERC20(currencyOut).balanceOf(address(this));
9
}
10
if (amountOut < minAmountOut) {
11
revert InsufficientOutputAmount(amountOut, minAmountOut);
12
}
There is no pre-call snapshot anywhere in the function, so whatever the contract already held is counted as this caller’s output. The entire
amount is then paid out to the caller-chosen recipient:
1
if (currencyOut == address(0)) {
2
(bool ok, ) = receiver.call{value: amountOut}("""");
3
if (!ok) revert InvalidValue();
4
} else {
5
// @audit-issue The full balance, not the swap's output, is sent to `receiver`.
6
IERC20(currencyOut).safeTransfer(receiver, amountOut);
7
}
This means that any leftover balance left in the contract will go to whoever calls the function next.
The same issue also corrupts the value the contract returns. The returned amountOut will be inflated by whatever leftover balance the
contract has, so a Weiroll command that uses the return value into a downstream step will request more tokens than the swap produced.
This contradicts the contract’s own documentation, which states that ""This contract holds no persistent state — tokens flow through the
same call frame they entered in"". This condition is not enforced anywhere in the code.
Recommendation(s): Consider snapshotting the balance of currencyOut (and of native ETH) before the Universal Router call and deriving
amountOut from the difference between the post-call and pre-call balances, so that only the value the swap actually produced is credited
and paid out.
Status: Fixed at commit 5467a92
Update from the client: Fixed in commit 5467a92. swapExactInSingle now snapshots its tokenOut balance before the Universal Router call
and credits only the post/pre difference, so a pre-existing balance is neither returned as amountOut nor paid to receiver, and minAmountOut
is enforced on the delta. VERSION bumped to 3 and the CREATE3 salt rotated for redeploy. Reproduced by new unit tests against a mock
Universal Router and a mainnet fork test that pre-funds the helper.
