File(s): src/CBNBRouter.sol
Description: Whitelisted depositors lock BNB by calling the deposit(...) function of the CBNBRouter contract. They send the BNB as
msg.value, and the router deducts two fees whose values are read from storage at execution time: the fixed cryptoFee and the proportional
protocolFeeBps. The router forwards the remainder to the vault. The relayer mints exactly this remainder as cBNB on Canton. The fees
stay in the router as feeBalance, which only the admin can collect.
1
function deposit(bytes calldata _recipient)
2
// ...
3
{
4
// ...
5
if (msg.value <= cryptoFee) {
6
revert InvalidCryptoFeeAmount();
7
}
8
9
// @audit-issue The fee values are read at execution time, and the depositor cannot bound them.
10
uint256 depositAmount = msg.value - cryptoFee;
11
12
uint256 protocolFee = _calculateFee(depositAmount);
13
14
uint256 amountAfterFees = depositAmount - protocolFee;
15
16
// @audit The only check on the net amount is the protocol-controlled minimum.
17
if (amountAfterFees < minAmount) {
18
revert AmountBelowMinimum();
19
}
20
21
uint256 totalFees = cryptoFee + protocolFee;
22
if (totalFees > 0) {
23
feeBalance += totalFees;
24
}
25
26
messageId = vault.deposit{value: amountAfterFees}(/* ... */);
27
}
The Manager Safe or the Admin Safe can change both fees at any time through the setFees(...) function. The change takes effect
immediately, with no delay. Only protocolFeeBps has an upper bound (10%); cryptoFee has none.
1
function setFees(uint256 _newCryptoFee, uint16 _newProtocolFeeBps)
2
external
3
onlyManagerOrAdmin
4
{
5
if (_newProtocolFeeBps > MAX_PROTOCOL_FEE_BPS) {
6
revert FeeTooHigh();
7
}
8
// @audit-issue There is no upper bound on the fixed fee and no delay before it applies.
9
cryptoFee = _newCryptoFee;
10
protocolFeeBps = _newProtocolFeeBps;
11
emit FeesUpdated(_newCryptoFee, _newProtocolFeeBps);
12
}
The deposit(...) function does not let the depositor state the fees they agreed to or the minimum amount they expect to have bridged.
The only check on the net amount is minAmount, and the protocol sets it, not the depositor. If a fee update is executed before a pending
deposit, whether in the same block or while the deposit is waiting to be mined, the deposit still succeeds. It is charged the new fees as
long as the remainder stays at or above minAmount. This needs no malicious intent: any routine fee change, such as the first move away
from the current 0 / 0 configuration, applies to every deposit that was signed under the old fees and is mined after the update.
Recommendation(s): Consider giving depositors a way to make sure that a deposit is only charged the fees they agreed to, for example
by binding each deposit to an expected fee or a minimum bridged amount. If the deployed contracts are to stay as they are, consider an
operational procedure that keeps fee updates from overlapping with pending deposits (for example, pausing deposits, waiting until pending
deposits are mined or dropped, and only then updating the fees), and communicating fee changes to whitelisted depositors before they
apply. Also consider placing an upper bound on cryptoFee.
Status: Acknowledged
Update from the client: Only Nuxaris-controlled addresses are whitelisted depositors, therefore this cannot affect external depositors.
