NM-1094 - Nuxaris-CBNB Contracts - SECURITY REVIEW
6.2
[Info] Deposit message IDs are only unique within a single vault deployment
File(s): src/CBNBVault.sol
Description: Whitelisted depositors bridge BNB to Canton by calling the deposit(...) function of the CBNBRouter contract, which forwards
the net amount to the deposit(...) function of the CBNBVault contract. The vault derives a messageId for every deposit and emits it in the
Deposit event. The off-chain relayer watches these events and, once the block is finalized, mints the same amount of cBNB on Canton.
The NatSpec describes messageId as the identifier ""used for idempotent processing"", and the README states that it ""is unique per deposit,
because it includes the vault nonce"".
1
/// @return messageId Unique message identifier used for idempotent processing
2
function deposit(...)
3
external
4
payable
5
onlyRouter
6
returns (bytes32 messageId)
7
{
8
// ...
9
messageId = _nextMessageId(_sender, msg.value, _dstChainID, _dstOutputToken, _recipient);
10
11
// @audit The relayer reads the messageId from this event.
12
emit Deposit(messageId, /* ... */);
13
}
The _nextMessageId(...) function computes the messageId as the hash of the depositor, the chain ID, the vault’s nonce, the net amount,
the destination and the recipient. The nonce is incremented on every deposit, so two deposits into the same vault always receive different
IDs, even when all their other parameters are identical.
1
function _nextMessageId(
2
address _sender,
3
uint256 _amount,
4
uint256 _dstChainID,
5
bytes32 _dstOutputToken,
6
bytes memory _recipient
7
) internal returns (bytes32) {
8
// @audit-issue The nonce is vault storage and starts at 0 in every new vault.
9
uint256 currentNonce = nonce++;
10
// @audit-issue None of the hashed fields identifies the vault instance.
11
return keccak256(
12
abi.encodePacked(
13
_sender,
14
block.chainid,
15
currentNonce,
16
_amount,
17
_dstChainID,
18
_dstOutputToken,
19
_recipient
20
)
21
);
22
}
However, this uniqueness only holds within a single vault instance. The nonce is a storage variable of the vault and starts at 0 in every
newly deployed vault, and none of the hashed fields identifies the vault itself. If the vault is redeployed on the same chain for any reason,
its nonce does not continue from the previous vault’s nonce. The k-th deposit into the new vault then receives exactly the same messageId
as the k-th deposit into the old vault whenever both have the same depositor, net amount, destination and recipient. This breaks the
uniqueness and idempotency guarantee of messageId, so any off-chain infrastructure or process that relies on it, such as the relayer,
would be working on a wrong assumption.
Recommendation(s): Consider accounting for this limitation in any future vault deployment or migration, so that deposit message IDs
remain unique across all vault instances.
Status: Acknowledged
Update from the client: Only Nuxaris-controlled addresses are whitelisted depositors, therefore this cannot affect external depositors.
