Description
When a customer takes their physical gold out of the vault, the system hands the certificate back to
them in perfect condition. Nothing marks it as used.
So a customer can walk out with the gold bar in one hand and a fully valid title deed for it in the other,
then hand that title deed straight back in and get a fresh batch of TROY for gold that is no longer there.
The tool to prevent this already exists. CertificatesNFT.burn destroys a certificate, only the vault can
call it, and it requires the vault to be holding the certificate, which is exactly the situation at the moment
of release. It is never called anywhere in src/ .
Two things make it worse:
Issuing a new certificate needs a certifier plus a one time use custody reference proving the bar
arrived. Re-depositing an old certificate needs none of that.
Because certificates are never burned, the bar's serial number stays reserved forever, so a bar that
genuinely comes back can never get a fresh properly checked certificate. Reusing the stale one is
the only way back in, which makes the dangerous action the routine one.
Impact
Total loss of the vault's gold.
TROY is a shared claim on a shared pool, and the operator releases whichever bar it likes. So this is not
a one time double count. One certificate can be recycled again and again to walk out with the entire
vault. Measured: a customer puts in one bar and leaves with four, the vault ends up completely empty,
and three honest customers hold tokens backed by nothing.
Throughout, the protocol's own health check onChainBackingMatchesSupply() reports everything is
fine, because the fake backing is added to both sides of the ledger at once.
Likelihood
Medium. An operator has to approve each deposit, but the operator has nothing to go on:
