Path
src/TroyToken.sol:59-65
src/CertificatesNFT.sol:111-117
Function Name
setVault
Description
The certificate contract and the TROY token each store a pointer to ""the vault"", which is the only
address allowed to create or destroy tokens and certificates. The owner can change that pointer at any
time. The only checks are that the new address is not empty and is a contract. Nothing verifies the old
vault has been emptied first.
The moment the pointer moves, the old vault becomes a locked box:
Releasing a stored bar requires destroying the customer's TROY, and that is restricted to whichever
vault the pointer currently names. The old vault is no longer that vault, so it fails.
The emergency ""rescue a stuck NFT"" function deliberately refuses to touch any certificate that is
backing tokens, so there is no back door.
The new vault starts blank. It has no record of the existing bars and there is no function to teach it
about them. The certificates are also still owned by the old vault, which cannot release them.
There is a second, quieter effect. Both assets have an emergency freeze on ordinary transfers, with an
exemption so the vault can still unwind pending customer requests. That exemption follows the pointer.
The instant it moves, the old vault, still holding customer money, loses its exemption.
Both shipped migration scripts do this pointer change with no safety check
( script/WireProtocol.s.sol:26-31 , script/DeployAndWireVaultSingleAdmin.s.sol:93-94 ).
Impact
Every gram of gold in the vault becomes unrecoverable, and every TROY token in circulation becomes
permanently unredeemable. There is no admin override and no upgrade that fixes it, because the
certificates are stuck in a contract that has lost its authority to move them.
This is unrecoverable by design. The rescue function's refusal to touch backing certificates rests on the
assumption that a normal withdrawal always exists as an exit, and that assumption is exactly what
breaks here.
