Medium Severity Issues
4. A price safety switch ships turned off and nothing in the
deployment turns it on
Resolved
Path
src/Exchange.sol:173-187
Function Name
_skewOk
Description
Every swap combines two price readings: the price of gold, and the price of the stablecoin being traded.
Each is separately checked for freshness, and neither may be older than about 65 minutes.
But two readings can both be individually fresh and still come from very different moments. A gold price
from right now paired with a stablecoin price from 65 minutes ago passes both checks. The contract has
a setting to limit that gap.
That setting is missing from the deployment configuration, is never assigned at startup, and so defaults
to zero. Zero means ""no limit, skip the check"". No deployment script turns it on. The go live checklist
script, which verifies oracle health, reserves and compliance before opening trading, never checks it
either.
The code's own comment describes what is being left open: combining prices from different moments is
""an arbitrage/manipulation surface even when each feed independently passes its own freshness
check"".
Impact
Traders can extract value from the exchange's reserves whenever either price is moving. The clearest
case is a stablecoin losing its peg. If the stablecoin's feed has not updated in the last hour, the
exchange still values it at one dollar while the market values it at ninety cents, and an arbitrageur buys
gold tokens with the cheap stablecoin at the stale rate.
Measured at the shipped settings, a 100,000 unit swap during a 10% depeg extracts about $9,500,
while the trading fee meant to price this risk collects $450. That is roughly 21 times the fee. The loss
falls on the exchange's liquidity providers.
Likelihood
Medium. Nothing needs to be hacked and no special access is required. It is an ordinary swap, available
to anyone, whenever conditions line up.
What holds it back from higher: the 65 minute freshness limit still caps how stale either price can get,
so the size of the opportunity is bounded by how much prices moved in that window. On a quiet day the
0.5% fee absorbs it. It takes a real depeg or a sharp gold move to become clearly profitable, so it is an
intermittent opportunity rather than a standing free option.
