CB-135,,I-01 Unbounded loop in batchMint can exceed the block gas limit,QuillAudits__CRVT_Smart_Contract_Audit_Report_-_QuillAudits.pdf,QuillAudits,crvt,2026-11-18,yes,no,2026-11,Informational,"Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
I-01
Unbounded loop in batchMint can exceed the block
gas limit
Acknowledged
Path
MintableToken.sol
Function Name
batchMint
Description
batchMint lets the owner mint tokens to many recipients in a single transaction. It loops over the whole
recipients array and calls the inherited OpenZeppelin _mint once per entry. Each call updates the
recipient's balance and the total supply and emits a Transfer event, and a mint to an address that has
never held the token costs more than 20,000 gas on its own, because it writes a new storage slot.
The function sets no upper limit on how many recipients one call can include, so its gas cost grows in a
straight line with the length of the arrays. Once a batch is large enough, it needs more gas than a single
block allows. Such a transaction cannot complete: it either fails at gas estimation or runs out of gas and
reverts in full, so no recipient in that batch receives tokens. Nothing in the contract tells the owner how
large a batch can safely be, so the owner only finds out by sending a transaction that fails, and a
distribution script that sends the full list in one call keeps failing until it is changed to split the list.
Impact
Low
No funds are lost and no outside user can trigger this, since only the owner can call batchMint . An
oversized batch simply fails, so the effect is a failed or delayed token distribution, possible wasted gas,
and the need to resend the list in smaller batches.
Likelihood
Low
Recommendation
Limit the number of recipients batchMint accepts per call, or split large distributions across several
smaller transactions.
13
www.quillaudits.com

CRVT - Audit Report
Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
I-02
Floating pragma may cause bytecode mismatch
Acknowledged
Path
MintableToken.sol
Description
MintableToken.sol doesn’t name one exact compiler version. It accepts version 0.8.20 or any later 0.8
release. The live token was actually compiled with 0.8.33, with the optimizer on at 200 runs and the
Prague EVM target, and the verified source matches the deployed bytecode exactly. That exact version
isn’t written in the contract itself. It exists only in the build settings used on deployment day.
Anyone who compiles this contract again may get a different compiler. That includes a team member
redeploying the token, deploying the same token on another chain, or an auditor reproducing the build.
Versions in that range differ in bug fixes, optimizer behaviour and the EVM version they target by default.
So a new build can produce different bytecode from the reviewed and deployed version, and may use
instructions that some chains don’t support.
The OpenZeppelin files the token imports also accept 0.8.20 and later. That’s normal for shared libraries,
which have to work with many projects. The final contract that actually gets deployed is the place to
lock one exact version.
Impact
Low
There is no impact on the live token, because its bytecode is already fixed on-chain. The risk only
applies to future deployments: a later build could use a different compiler from the one that was
reviewed and tested.
Likelihood
Low
Recommendation
In any future deployment, set the contract’s compiler version to exactly 0.8.33, the version the live token
was built with, rather than a range. Keep the same version, optimizer settings and EVM target fixed in the
project’s build configuration, so every build matches the reviewed one.
14
www.quillaudits.com

CRVT - Audit Report
Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
I-03
Missing explicit zero address check in batchMint
Acknowledged
Path
MintableToken.sol
Function Name
batchMint
Description
batchMint lets the owner mint tokens to many recipients in a single transaction. It takes a recipients
array and an amounts array, checks that both have the same length, and then loops over them, calling
the inherited OpenZeppelin _mint once for each recipient and amount pair. Only the owner can call it,
because it is protected by onlyOwner .
Inside the loop, batchMint does not check whether a recipient is the zero address before minting. It
relies entirely on OpenZeppelin's _mint , which already reverts when the receiver is the zero address. So
if the owner includes the zero address anywhere in recipients , the whole batch reverts and no tokens
are minted to anyone, which means tokens cannot actually be minted to the zero address today. The
gap is that this rule is not stated in the contract itself: a reader has to know the library's internal
behaviour to see that the case is handled, and the function gives no sign that zero address recipients
were considered.
Impact
Low
There is no loss of funds and no way for anyone to exploit this. Tokens cannot be minted to the zero
address, because the inherited _mint already blocks it and the whole batch reverts. The effect is
limited to code clarity in an owner only function.
Likelihood
Low
Recommendation
Add an explicit zero address check for each recipient in batchMint before minting.
15
