CrackerSwap Audit Report
High Severity Issues
Any caller can force a healthy launch into permanent RECOVERY from
an unlocked PoolManager frame 
That guard is correct in isolation, and the attacker can enter its rejecting state at will. In v4-core,
PoolManager.unlock(bytes) is external and permissionless: it sets Lock.IS_UNLOCKED_SLOT
0xc090fc4683624cfc3884e9d8de5eca132f2d0ec062aff75d43c0465d5ceeab23, byte-
identical to the module's hardcoded constant to true, then calls unlockCallback on msg.sender,
who controls the whole frame and may make arbitrary external calls. A callback that settles no
currency leaves NonzeroDeltaCount at zero, so the outer unlock() succeeds.
The attacker therefore calls curve.enterRecovery() from inside their own unlockCallback. migrate
reverts PoolManagerUnlocked(), the bare catch swallows it, and a launch whose migration is
perfectly healthy is moved to terminal RECOVERY.
The root cause is not that the PoolManager rejects the migration that behaviour is correct. It is that
enterRecovery() lets an attacker manufacture that expected revert and then reads it as a genuine
migration failure.
In v4-periphery, modifyLiquiditiesWithoutUnlock acquires the PositionManager’s own lock without
unlocking the underlying core PoolManager. During this state, SWEEP can be directed to an
attacker-controlled currency and recipient, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code while
the core PoolManager remains locked.
This execution path can reach enterRecovery, where settlement subsequently fails with
ContractLocked.
Impact
Permanent, irreversible destruction of a healthy launch, at a profit to the attacker:
- the entire LP allocation plus unsold curve supply is burned (BondingCurve.sol:401-402);
- graduation is off for good no V4 pool is ever opened, and graduate() reverts WrongPhase;
- the launch drops into the value-redistributing pro-rata RECOVERY claim, which transfers value
from late buyers to early buyers.
Likelihood
Medium. Two preconditions: the curve must be CLOSED, and RECOVERY_DELAY (7 days) must have
elapsed. The attacker can manufacture the CLOSED state themselves (see finding 5), but cannot
prevent any third party from calling the permissionless graduate() during those 7 days, which
defeats the attack entirely. The exposure is therefore a launch that sits deferred and unattended
for a week.
