CB-140,FR-346,3. Idle-pool TWAP is cheaply settable by a single trader and feeds the irreversible FDV ratchet,QuillAudits__Cracker_Smart_Contract_Audit_Report_-_QuillAudits.pdf,QuillAudits,cracker,2026-08-31,no,no,2026-09,Medium,"Medium Severity Issues
Medium Severity Issues
3. Idle-pool TWAP is cheaply settable by a single
trader and feeds the irreversible FDV ratchet
Partially Resolved
Path
contracts/hooks/AntiSnipeHook.sol, contracts/LPFeeRatchet.sol, 
contracts/TokenSplitter.sol
Function Name
AntiSnipeHook._beforeSwap(), LPFeeRatchet.poke(), 
TokenSplitter.ratchetDown()
The FDV that decides the creator's permanent LP-fee tier is a TWAP built from AntiSnipeHook's
cumulative-tick accumulator. That accumulator advances only on swaps, and each swap credits
the pre-swap standing tick across the whole interval since the previous swap
(AntiSnipeHook.sol:164-183):
LPFeeRatchet.poke requires only that the observed span between two recorded timestamps be at
least 24 hours (TWAP_WINDOW). It does not require any trading activity inside that span
(LPFeeRatchet.sol:152-186).
On an idle or thinly-traded pool a single actor therefore defines the entire window: swap once to
park the tick, poke to open the window, wait 24 hours untouched there is no arbitrage cost on an
idle pool then flush with one tiny swap that accumulates tick × 24h at the parked price, and poke
again to commit the tier. TokenSplitter.ratchetDown is monotone-down and irreversible
(TokenSplitter.sol:158-167), and the LP position is locked forever with no exit path in LPLocker.
36
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
3. Idle-pool TWAP is cheaply settable by a single trader and feeds the
irreversible FDV ratchet 
Impact
An attacker can park the tick upward across a 24-hour idle window to force a permanent ratchet
from 7000 bps toward 4000 bps, griefing the creator on all future volume. Conversely a creator or
ally can park the tick downward to indefinitely retain a top tier the true FDV would not justify,
permanently diverting LP fees from the treasury. Either direction is a permanent, one-time-
manipulable 30-percentage-point misallocation of the creator↔treasury LP-fee split for the entire
life of the locked position.
Likelihood
Medium. Requires an idle or thinly-traded pool and enough capital to move and hold the tick, but
there is no ongoing cost once the pool is idle, and the creator-ally direction is directly profitable.
POC
Sequence, all calls permissionless:
- Swap once to park the tick at the desired level.
- LPFeeRatchet.poke(launchToken) anchors the observation at (tickCumulative, lastObservedAt).
- Wait 24 hours. No swaps occur, so the accumulator does not move and there is nothing to
arbitrage.
- Execute one minimal swap. _beforeSwap credits parkedTick × 86400 in a single write.
- LPFeeRatchet.poke(launchToken) span = 86400 ≥ TWAP_WINDOW, so the mean equals the parked
tick exactly and the tier commits.
Recommendation
Require evidence of activity inside the window a minimum swap count, or a freshness bound on
lastObservedAt relative to block.timestamp. Cap the committable span (span <= k * TWAP_WINDOW,
reopening the window rather than committing when exceeded). Consider a median-of-samples
rather than a single mean. Alternatively make the ratchet re-entrant upward with a fresh window so
that one manipulated window cannot be permanent.
CrackerSwap Team’s Comment
Partially resolved; the residual is accepted. The cheap half of this issue is closed by the fix for
issue 6, which snapshots FDV supply when the pool is armed. The residual is that, on a pool with no
trading, observed time does not advance, so one actor who parks the tick and later closes the
interval defines the whole window. This is inherent to a TWAP on an idle market. An activity floor is
defeated by dust swaps, and a volume floor priced high enough to deter manipulation would also
stop quiet-but-valuable launches from ratcheting at all, with every such failure favouring the
creator at the protocol's expense. This is recorded in ARCHITECTURE.md as the standing
assumption to attack.
(continued)
37
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
Medium Severity Issues
4. LPLocker.collect() and LPFeeRatchet.poke() are an
unordered permissionless pair, so an accrued LP-fee pool is
priced at whichever lands first
Resolved
Path
contracts/LPLocker.sol, contracts/TokenSplitter.sol, 
contracts/LPFeeRatchet.sol
Function Name
LPLocker.collect(), TokenSplitter._credit(), LPFeeRatchet.poke()
LPLocker.collect(tokenId) is permissionless (LPLocker.sol:140-165). It harvests accrued Uniswap
V4 fees and forwards them to TokenSplitter.depositETH / depositERC20 under Stream.LP_FEES.
The splitter resolves the creator's share with lpCreatorBpsOf(launchToken) evaluated at deposit
time, not at accrual time (TokenSplitter.sol:187-207):
LPFeeRatchet.poke(), which lowers that share as FDV rises, is also permissionless. The applied
split therefore depends entirely on which of two independent, unprivileged calls lands first. A
creator bot can watch FDV approach a threshold and collect() immediately before anyone poke()s,
crediting every fee accrued up to that point at the old, higher bps.
The material point is the size of the pool being repriced. Nothing obliges anyone to collect() before
the first poke(). Both calls are unpaid, so there is no economic pressure on any third party to make
them, and the party with the strongest incentive the creator profits from delay. The pool priced at
the stale tier is therefore the entire uncollected backlog since graduation, which for a successful
launch is the largest fee accrual of its life.
38
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
4. LPLocker.collect() and LPFeeRatchet.poke() are an unordered
permissionless pair, so an accrued LP-fee pool is priced at whichever
lands first 
Impact
The creator captures accrued LP fees at up to the pre-ratchet share 70% instead of 60%, 50%, or
40% on the whole uncollected backlog. This is a permanent misallocation of protocol revenue
against the schedule the ratchet exists to enforce, recurring at each of the three thresholds on
every launch. No user principal is at risk and no user is denied an action, which is what keeps this
below High.
Likelihood
High. Both legs are permissionless, neither requires capital or a privileged role, the ordering is
trivially automatable, and the profitable ordering is the one the creator wants.
