Low Severity Issues
Low Severity Issues
5. A closing buy made from an unlocked PoolManager
frame defers settlement and freezes trading
Acknowledged
Path
contracts/BondingCurve.sol
Function Name
_buy(), settleGraduation(), graduate(), enterRecovery()
_buy's settlement catch treats only an empty revert reason as fatal (BondingCurve.sol:326-330);
any revert carrying data emits GraduationDeferred and leaves the curve CLOSED.
PoolManagerUnlocked() is a four-byte custom error, so a closing buy executed from inside a
permissionless PoolManager.unlock() callback takes the deferral branch instead of graduating
atomically. The attacker needs only the remaining headroom to the graduation target; _buy
clamps the buy to the target and refunds the excess.
Impact
The launch parks at CLOSED, where both buy() and sell() revert WrongPhase, so trading is frozen
and no pool exists. On its own this is recoverable, because graduate() is permissionless and
unsticks it.
Likelihood
Medium. Permissionless, costs only the residual headroom plus gas, and is repeatable against
any launch approaching its target.
Recommendation
Fixed by the same change as issue 2: distinguish temporarily-unavailable reverts from genuine
settlement failure rather than treating every non-empty reason as a deferral.
CrackerSwap Team’s Comment
_buy deliberately still defers settlement rather than reverting, because a caller invoking the curve
from inside its own unlock callback would otherwise have a legitimate purchase fail. The harm of
this issue was that it set up issue 2; with issue 2 resolved, a parked launch is recoverable by
anyone through the permissionless graduate(). A two-line hardening using migrationReady() has
been drafted but not applied, pending a decision on the in-unlock-caller trade-off.
40
