Low Severity Issues
6. FDV fee-tier accounting reads live totalSupply, which any
holder can shrink by burning
Resolved
Path
contracts/LPFeeRatchet.sol, contracts/LaunchToken.sol
Function Name
fdvFromTick(), fdvOf(), poke(), LaunchToken.burn()
fdvFromTick and fdvOf compute FDV from the token's live totalSupply() (LPFeeRatchet.sol:144-149),
and LaunchToken.burn lets any holder destroy their own balance (LaunchToken.sol:43-45). A large
holder, in practice the creator, can burn to shrink totalSupply, lowering computed FDV below a
ratchet threshold, so poke() observes a tier that does not justify ratcheting down.
Two properties make this sharper than a static ""sit below the threshold"" strategy.
The burn does not merely postpone a commit; it destroys the window. The observed >= current
branch re-anchors the observation rather than leaving it open (LPFeeRatchet.sol:177-181):
if (observed >= current) {
    observations[launchToken] = Observation(cumulativeNow, observedNow);
    emit ObservationStarted(launchToken, cumulativeNow, observedNow);
    return;
}
A burn landing in the same transaction as a poke, front-running a keeper's committing poke,
therefore erases a matured 24-hour observation. The keeper must open a fresh window and wait
another full 24 hours of observed, swap-advanced time.
41
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
Low Severity Issues
6. FDV fee-tier accounting reads live totalSupply, which any holder can
shrink by burning 
The cost is the overshoot, not a fixed share of supply. To hold FDV below a threshold T when the
price implies F, the required burn is exactly supply * (1 - T/F). Near a threshold that is negligible.
Impact
Suppression of the one-way FDV ratchet: the creator retains a higher split than the schedule
intends. The attack costs the attacker their burned holdings, so it is rational only where the fee-
share gain exceeds the burn cost, which near a threshold it comfortably does.
Likelihood
Medium near a tier boundary, low once the price has run decisively past one. A sustained rally
makes suppression cost the full overshoot: a doubling of FDV past the threshold costs half the
supply.
Recommendation
Snapshot totalSupply at graduation, recorded by the splitter or the ratchet at migration time, and
use the snapshot for FDV instead of the live value. This closes both the static suppression and the
window-destruction front-run in one change.
(continued)
