CrackerSwap Audit Report
Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
13. Curve initialisation stamps every economic parameter
but emits no event
Acknowledged
Path
contracts/BondingCurve.sol
Function Name
initialize()
The one-shot initialisation of every per-launch economic parameter, covering the virtual quote,
curve and LP supply, graduation target, both buy caps, the trade fee, the gate, the module and the
derived virtual token reserve, together with the transition into TRADING, emits nothing
(BondingCurve.sol:151-198). The only launch-level signal is the factory's LaunchCreated, which
does not carry the stamped parameters.
Impact
Indexers and front ends cannot reconstruct a curve's immutable configuration from logs alone
and must read storage instead. No on-chain value impact.
Likelihood
Certain that the gap exists on every launch; impact is observability-only.
Recommendation
Emit an Initialized event from initialize carrying the full parameter payload and the derived virtual
token reserve.
CrackerSwap Team’s Comment
Every parameter initialize stamps is immutable and readable from storage, so a backfilling indexer
can reconstruct the configuration without logs. A wide event would add real gas at createLaunch
for data already on chain, and would invert the explicit conclusion of commit 9882486, which
moved the protocol toward on-chain state instead of log replay.
50
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
14. Curve initialisation has no caller restriction
Acknowledged
Path
contracts/BondingCurve.sol
Function Name
initialize()
initialize() is externally callable by anyone; its only guard is the one-shot phase check
(BondingCurve.sol:151-152). Unlike seedBuy, which is gated to the factory, there is no msg.sender
== factory restriction. Today this is unreachable: createLaunch clones and initialises in a single
transaction (TokenFactory.sol:167-190), so no front-run window exists.
Impact
None currently. The gap becomes live only if a future factory version separates the clone and the
initialisation into two transactions, at which point an attacker could initialise a freshly cloned
curve with parameters of their choosing before the factory does.
Likelihood
Very low. Latent; depends on a future code change rather than on any current state.
Recommendation
Add the explicit gate, so the invariant is enforced by the contract rather than by the caller's
construction:
function initialize(InitParams calldata p) external {
    if (msg.sender != factory) revert Unauthorized();
    if (phase != Phase.UNINITIALIZED) revert AlreadyInitialized();
Note that factory is written by initialize itself, so the check must read the parameter rather than
storage, or the factory address must be fixed at clone time. Preserving the atomic clone-plus-
initialise invariant in any factory rewrite is the alternative if the storage ordering makes the gate
awkward.
51
www.quillaudits.com

CrackerSwap Audit Report
