Informational Severity Issues
15. The anti-snipe reservation guard is silently skipped
once the decay window closes
Acknowledged
Path
contracts/hooks/AntiSnipeHook.sol
Function Name
_beforeAddLiquidity(), seeded()
Both the seeding writes and the LaunchWindowClosed guard live entirely inside the window check
at AntiSnipeHook.sol:142-147. The false branch, meaning the window has elapsed or the pool was
never armed, writes nothing and enforces no reservation. seeded() therefore reports false for a
pool seeded after the window, and no post-window add is gated. This is unreachable today: the
only add path is the migration module, which initialises the pool and mints atomically in the same
transaction, so the write always fires in-window.
Impact
None in the current call graph. It becomes a real reservation bypass if a second liquidity-add route
to a hooked pool is ever introduced, and it misleads integrators reading seeded().
Likelihood
Very low. Latent; requires a future second add path.
Recommendation
Document the invariant that all seeding happens in the migration transaction. If another add path
is introduced, hoist the reservation logic out of the window check so the guard cannot be skipped.
CrackerSwap Team’s Comment
Free liquidity after the decay window is the intended behaviour and is asserted in the live-fork suite
(""lets liquidity in freely once the window has elapsed""). The only residue is that seeded() would
read false for a pool seeded after the window, which cannot occur because migration initialises
the pool and mints liquidity atomically.
53
