CB-156,,"H-01 Buyers are mispriced on every native-coin purchase via hardcoded $1,700 price in _cryptoValues",QuillAudits__Lendary_Smart_Contract_Audit_Report_-_QuillAudits.pdf,QuillAudits,lendary,2026-09-23,no,no,2026-10,High,"High Severity Issues
High Severity Issues
H-01
Buyers are mispriced on every native-coin purchase via
hardcoded $1,700 price in _cryptoValues
Resolved
Path
contracts/ICO.sol
Function Name
_cryptoValues(), calculateTokens(), buyTokens()
Description
ICO sells LRY across time-bound phases at a fixed USD price per token. Phase 1 is set up in
initialize at a price of 0.075 USD and a limit of 50,000,000 LRY. A KYC-approved buyer calls
buyTokens and picks a payment type: 1 for the chain's native coin (the NatSpec says BNB/ETH), 2 for
USDT, 3 for USDC. For native payments, buyTokens replaces _buyAmount with msg.value and calls
calculateTokens . That function gets a USD rate and a decimal divisor from _cryptoValues , multiplies
the payment by the rate, and divides by the divisor to get _amountGivenInUsd .
_calculateTokensInternal then divides that USD figure by the phase price, or by the buyer's
userCustomPrice override if one is set, to decide how many LRY to send. buyTokens adds the result to
totalTokenSold and the USD figure to totalUSDRaised , forwards the native coin to receiverAddress ,
and transfers the LRY to the buyer straight away.
The contract stores an ETHOracle reference, set in initialize , and the _cryptoValues NatSpec says
it returns an oracle price. However, for payment type 1, _cryptoValues never reads the oracle. It returns
a fixed constant of 1,700 USD (scaled by 10^8) for one whole native coin, and the oracle call is
commented out. Stablecoin payments are handled correctly at a 1:1 USD rate. So every native-coin
purchase is valued as if the native coin is always worth exactly 1,700 USD, whatever its real market price,
on whatever chain the contract is deployed to. There is no way to correct this after deployment,
because no setter updates the rate and no other code path reads ETHOracle .
16
www.quillaudits.com

Lendary - Audit Report
High Severity Issues
H-01
Buyers are mispriced on every native-coin purchase via hardcoded
$1,700 price in _cryptoValues 
When the native coin trades below 1,700 USD, every native buyer gets more LRY than they paid for, and
the protocol bears the shortfall. If the contract runs on BNB Chain, as the NatSpec suggests, and BNB
trades at 600 USD, one BNB buys 22,666 LRY at the 0.075 USD phase price instead of the correct 8,000
LRY. That is about 2.83 times the fair amount. Any KYC-approved buyer can repeat this freely. Taking the
full 50,000,000 LRY allocation, priced at 3,750,000 USD, would cost roughly 2,206 BNB, about
1,323,000 USD. The buyer then receives the tokens at once and can sell them below the sale price. The
same applies on Ethereum whenever ETH drops below 1,700 USD. When the native coin trades above
1,700 USD, honest buyers lose instead. At an ETH price of 3,400 USD, one ETH buys 22,666 LRY rather
than 45,333 LRY, so the buyer loses half the value they sent. In both cases totalUSDRaised and the
BuyTokenDetail event report USD amounts that do not match the value actually received.
So no native-coin purchase is ever priced at market. Depending on which way the price has moved,
either the protocol sells its token allocation at a deep discount or buyers overpay for the tokens they
receive.
Impact
High
When the native coin trades below 1,700 USD, buyers can drain the sale allocation for a fraction of its
value. With BNB at 600 USD, the full 50,000,000 LRY allocation (3,750,000 USD at the phase price)
goes for about 1,323,000 USD. The protocol permanently loses about 2,427,000 USD of sale proceeds,
and the buyer captures that difference as profit. When the native coin trades above 1,700 USD, every
native-coin buyer loses value directly, for example 50% of each payment at an ETH price of 3,400 USD.
Every native-coin sale also corrupts the protocol's totalUSDRaised accounting.
Likelihood
Medium
Recommendation
Price native-coin purchases from a live oracle feed for the correct asset on the deployment chain, and
validate that the answer is positive, fresh and complete before using it.
(continued)
