Lendary - Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
Medium Severity Issues
M-04
Future upgrades can corrupt Staking, ICO and Vesting
state via unreserved storage in inherited base contracts
Resolved
Path
Staking.sol, ICO.sol, Vesting.sol, KycSignatureGate.sol, MetaTransaction.sol
Function Name
initialize()
Description
LendaryStaking , LendaryICO and LendaryVesting are built to sit behind
LendaryTransparentUpgradeableProxy . Each has an initializer-guarded initialize and disables
initializers in its constructor. Behind a transparent proxy, all state lives in the proxy's storage, and every
new implementation must read the existing slots exactly the way the previous one wrote them. Solidity
lays out state variables one after another in inheritance order: base contracts first, from the leftmost
parent, then the child's own variables. For example, LendaryStaking inherits Ownable , Pausable ,
ReentrancyGuard , Initializable , KycSignatureGate and BasicMetaTransaction , in that order, and
only then declares its own state. LendaryICO and LendaryVesting follow the same pattern with
Ownable , ReentrancyGuard , KycSignatureGate , Initializable and BasicMetaTransaction .
None of the parent contracts reserve space for future growth. KycSignatureGate declares kycSigner ,
kycNonces and _isSignatureUsed in plain sequential slots, with no __gap array and no namespaced
(ERC-7201) storage. BasicMetaTransaction does the same with its private nonces mapping. All three
contracts also inherit the standard, non-upgradeable OpenZeppelin Ownable , Pausable and
ReentrancyGuard rather than the upgradeable variants. Those standard versions are not meant to keep
a stable layout across library upgrades, and moving to the upgradeable variants later would change the
layout too. So the slot position of every child variable depends on the exact variable count of every
parent at the time of deployment.
25
www.quillaudits.com

Lendary - Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
M-04
Future upgrades can corrupt Staking, ICO and Vesting state via
unreserved storage in inherited base contracts 
In practice, if a later version adds a single variable to KycSignatureGate , for example a signer expiry or
a second signer, every variable that comes after it moves by one slot. In LendaryStaking , that includes
the stakes mapping, _totalStakedByUserId , _totalObligated , tierConfig ,
monthlyAutoCompoundEnabled and governanceBonusUntil . In LendaryICO it includes phaseInfo ,
totalTokenSold and receiverAddress . The new implementation would read the old values from the
wrong slots. Staked principals, reward obligations, tier configuration and sale accounting would decode
as unrelated numbers or addresses, and the next state-changing call would write that corrupted state
back permanently. The same thing happens if a parent is reordered or removed, if a child variable is
inserted anywhere except at the end, or if the OpenZeppelin dependency is upgraded and changes the
layout of Ownable , Pausable or ReentrancyGuard .
The contracts therefore have no structural protection against a routine-looking change to a shared base
contract. A single upgrade that touches the inheritance chain can silently corrupt the state that tracks
every staker's principal and rewards and every buyer's sale accounting.
Impact
Medium
An incompatible upgrade would corrupt live storage in whichever contract is upgraded. In
LendaryStaking that means every position's principal, rewardAccrued and timestamps, plus the
protocol's obligation accounting. This can lead to wrong payouts, failed withdrawals or locked staked
LRY, and it cannot be undone once the corrupted state is written. The risk only becomes real when an
upgrade changes the inheritance chain or the order of variables.
[...excerpt ends]"
