Lendary - Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
Medium Severity Issues
M-06
Stakers lose or gain rewards they already earned
because _refreshMultipliersOfUserId changes the whale
multiplier before accruing them
Resolved
Path
contracts/Staking.sol, contracts/libraries/StakingLibrary.sol
Function Name
_refreshMultipliersOfUserId(), _openStakePosition(), _settleCooldownExit(), 
_accrueStakeFor(), partialUnstake()
Description
Staking scales each position's reward rate with an anti-whale multiplier based on how much the
owning userId has staked across all its positions and linked wallets. StakingLibrary.
computeWhaleMultiplier returns 1.0x while _totalStakedByUserId is at or below 1% of LRY total
supply. Between 1% and 2% it falls linearly to 0.7x, and above 2% it stays at 0.7x. Each position stores
its own copy in whaleMultiplierBps . Rewards are not streamed. A position accrues lazily, only when
_accrueStakeFor runs for that stake id. It then charges simple interest from lastAccrualTimestamp to
now on principal, multiplied by the APY and by whatever whaleMultiplierBps holds at that moment. A
user action only calls _accrueStakeFor for the one stake id being acted on ( stake calls it for none), so
a user's other positions can go unaccrued for months.
Every action that changes the aggregate calls _refreshMultipliersOfUserId afterwards. This happens
when _openStakePosition opens a new stake, when _settleCooldownExit pays out a withdrawal or
accelerated exit, when partialUnstake or unstakeEarly burns a penalty, and after each compounding
step inside _accrueStakeFor . _refreshMultipliersOfUserId loops over every position of the userId
that still has principal and overwrites its whaleMultiplierBps with the newly computed value. It does
not accrue any of those positions first. Their lastAccrualTimestamp stays where it was. So the next
time each sibling position accrues, the whole interval since its last checkpoint is priced at the new
multiplier, even though the aggregate during most of that interval justified a different rate. A change
that should only affect future yield is applied backwards to yield already earned.
