Low Severity Issues
L-01
Anyone can revert batchClaimFor by front-running it
with claimTokens for one entry
Resolved
Path
contracts/Vesting.sol
Function Name
batchClaimFor(), claimTokens()
Description
Vesting releases LRY to registered beneficiaries in scheduled periods per vesting category. A
beneficiary's claimable amount for a category comes from tokensToBeClaimed , which compares the
claim period the schedule has reached with the periods already claimed. Anyone can call claimTokens
for any beneficiary and category. It takes no signature and no access control, and the tokens always go
to the beneficiary's address. To save gas and operations, batchClaimFor lets a caller, normally a
backend keeper, process up to 25 beneficiary and category pairs in one transaction. It checks the array
lengths and then calls the shared internal _claimForInternal for each pair in turn.
_claimForInternal is strict. It reverts with UserNotRegistered if the beneficiary has no allocation in
that category. It then reads the claimable amount from tokensToBeClaimed , and if that amount is zero
it reverts with AllTokensClaimed or NothingToClaim . Only when the amount is non-zero does it update
claimedAmount and the claim count and transfer the tokens. batchClaimFor does not catch or skip
these reverts, so one entry with nothing to claim reverts the whole transaction and rolls back every
other claim in the batch. The function's documentation tells callers to pre-check each entry with
tokensToBeClaimed before building the batch. That check happens off-chain when the batch is built,
and nothing stops the state from changing before the batch is mined.
An attacker watching the mempool sees a keeper's batchClaimFor covering 25 beneficiaries and front-
runs it with a claimTokens call for any one of those beneficiaries and categories. The attacker's call
pays that beneficiary their current period and advances their claim count. When the keeper's batch
executes, that entry now has a claimable amount of zero, _claimForInternal reverts with
NothingToClaim , and the other 24 claims are undone. The keeper pays gas for the failed batch, has to
rebuild it and resubmit it, and the attacker can do the same against the next submission. No attacker is
needed either: if any beneficiary in the batch calls claimTokens for themselves shortly before the batch
is mined, the batch fails the same way.
