CB-179,FR-362,Tokens sent to the token contract's own address are locked forever,QuillAudits__TBC_Token_Audit_Report-QuillAudits.pdf,QuillAudits,tbc-token,2026-10-07,no,no,2026-10,Informational,"Informational Severity Issues
Informational Severity Issues
Tokens sent to the token contract's own address are
locked forever
Acknowledged
Path
contracts/TBCToken.sol
Function Name
transfer(), transferFrom() (inherited from ERC20) and batchTransfer()
Description
TBCToken accepts transfers to its own contract address. OpenZeppelin's _transfer only rejects the zero
address as a recipient, and TBCToken adds no check of its own, so transfer, transferFrom and
batchTransfer all send tokens to address(this) without reverting.
Once tokens are at that address, nothing can move them again. Moving tokens out of an address needs
either a call from that address or an allowance from it. The token contract never calls transfer or
approve on its own behalf, and because there is no owner, there is no rescue function and no way to add
one later. The tokens stay counted in totalSupply but can never be spent.
This is a common mistake with ERC-20 tokens in general, because the token's contract address is the
one holders copy most often, for example to add the token to a wallet or to look it up on a block explorer.
It can also happen in bulk if the token's own address ends up in a batchTransfer recipient list.
Impact
None for the contract or for other holders. The only tokens at risk are the ones a holder sends to the
wrong address by mistake, and those cannot be recovered.
Likelihood
Low. It needs a holder to make a mistake. No one else can cause it.
