CB-180,,M-01 Changing the USDC address breaks per-offering accounting,QuillAudits__Tradevu_Smart_Contract_Audit_Report_-_QuillAudits.pdf,QuillAudits,tradevu,2026-09-30,no,no,2026-11,Medium,"Medium Severity Issues
Medium Severity Issues
M-01
Changing the USDC address breaks per-offering
accounting
Resolved
Path
TradevuEscrow.sol
Function Name
updateUSDC
Description
The escrow tracks money in orderPool[token] , settlementPool[token] and totalEscrowed . Each is
a plain number with no record of which USDC contract it is denominated in. updateUSDC swaps the
usdc pointer and migrates nothing.
After the switch, every ledger still says the escrow owes the same number of units, but the units now
mean a different token. The old balance sits in the old USDC contract with no code path to pay it out.
Every later refund, redemption and sweep calls usdc.safeTransfer on the new token, so it pays from
whatever the escrow holds of the new token. That balance belongs to offerings that were funded after
the switch. A refund for a pre-switch order therefore either reverts (balance too small) or takes money
from a post-switch offering.
```solidity
function updateUSDC(address usdc_) external onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {
    require(usdc_ != address(0), ""zero address"");
    usdc = IERC20(usdc_);
}
Impact
High
Pre-switch investors cannot be refunded or redeemed, because their money is in a token the escrow no
longer touches. Post-switch investors can be short-changed when pre-switch claims are paid out of the
new balance, so one offering's loss lands on another. The view unaccountedBalance() computes usdc.
balanceOf(this) - totalEscrowed and reverts on underflow once the new balance is below
totalEscrowed , which makes the solvency check unusable exactly when it is needed. The function
emits no event, so the change is also invisible off-chain. The size of the loss is every unsettled balance
at the moment of the switch.
16
www.quillaudits.com

Tradevu - Audit Report
Medium Severity Issues
M-01
Changing the USDC address breaks per-offering accounting
Likelihood
Low
Low. Only DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE can call it. The realistic trigger is an honest migration (a USDC contract
change, or a typo in the new address) done while offerings are live, not an attack
POC
Owner calls updateUSDC without migrating/clearing earlier balances.
Recommendation
Remove the function. If a migration path is required, allow it only when nothing is owed, check that the
new token matches, and emit an event:
event USDCUpdated(address indexed oldUsdc, address indexed newUsdc);
function updateUSDC(address usdc_) external onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {
    require(usdc_ != address(0), ""zero address"");
    require(totalEscrowed == 0, ""funds held"");
    require(
        IERC20Metadata(usdc_).decimals() == IERC20Metadata(address(usdc)).decimals(),
        ""decimals differ""
    );
    emit USDCUpdated(address(usdc), usdc_);
    usdc = IERC20(usdc_);
}
Put the admin behind a multisig and a timelock, so a switch is announced before it takes effect.
Tradevu Team's Comment
Applied the recommended fix.
