Tradevu - Audit Report
Low Severity Issues
Low Severity Issues
L-01
Any caller can take another investor's order ID first
Acknowledged
Path
TradevuEscrow.sol
Function Name
placeOrder()
Description
Both the used-ID map and the order map are keyed by a string the caller picks.
The ID is not tied to the caller. An attacker who learns an ID before the victim's transaction lands places
a minimum order with it. The victim's call then reverts with ActionIdAlreadyUsed , and the order under
that ID belongs to the attacker. The ID can leak from a public mempool (the victim's pending
placeOrder calldata), from the backend's API, or from a predictable ID scheme.
```solidity
if (actionIdUsed[actionID]) revert ActionIdAlreadyUsed();
...
orders[actionID] = Order({ token: token, user: msg.sender, amount: amount, resolved: false });
actionIdUsed[actionID] = true;
Impact
Low
The victim does not lose funds, because their transaction reverts, and can retry with a fresh ID. The
harm is denied service for a chosen investor.
Likelihood
Medium
Medium, if IDs are guessable or visible before inclusion.
Recommendation
Make the stored key depend on the caller, so two users can never collide:
mapping(bytes32 => Order) public orders;
function placeOrder(address token, uint256 amount, bytes32 id) external nonReentrant {
    bytes32 key = keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, id));
    if (orders[key].token != address(0)) revert ActionIdAlreadyUsed();
