Redemption rounding underpays the last redeemers
Resolved
Path
TradevuEscrow.sol
Description
When redemption is funded, the distribution fee is taken once on the whole deposit, rounded down, and
the remainder becomes the pool. At redemption the fee is taken again on each holder's share, also
rounded down. Each per-holder rounding leaves up to one USDC base unit of fee uncollected, so total
payouts can exceed the pool by up to one base unit per holder.
Redemption is first come, first served. When the pool runs out, the next holder is paid whatever is left,
has only a proportional part of their tokens burned, and keeps the rest. Those leftover tokens cannot be
redeemed for anything, because the pool is empty.
Separately, a holder whose gross share rounds down to zero (trueBillPrice × balance 0
await expect(ctx.escrow.connect(ctx.carol).redeemBillTokens(""bill-1""))
.to.changeTokenBalance(ctx.usdc, ctx.carol, 0n);
expect(await offering.token.balanceOf(ctx.carol.address)).to.equal(0n);
});
`
Impact
Low
Low. The amounts are rounding dust, at most one USDC base unit per holder, but they fall entirely on the
last redeemers instead of being shared.
Likelihood
High
High. It happens naturally whenever holders' shares do not divide evenly by the fee rate. A holder could
deliberately split balances to create it, but that costs far more in gas than it shifts.
[...excerpt ends]"
