Issue L-2: Cancelling a queued redemption after cash
was sourced from an impaired strategy moves the
exit loss onto the other LPs [RESOLVED]
Source: https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-09-maple-sept-7th-2026/issues/51
Summary
MapleStrategyManager::processRedemption() burns shares at the exit rate without
reducing impairedAssets, and MapleWithdrawalManager::removeSharesById() lets the
request owner cancel at any point before processRedemptions(), so the value forfeited to
fund one LP's exit is carried by every LP in the pool instead of the LP who requested it.
Vulnerability Detail
Pricing the strategy manager exit net of the impairment is intended: the pool giving up
shares carries its part of the loss, and the queue then pays the exiting LP at the pool's
own impaired exit price, so the forfeit lands on the LP whose request caused it. What is
missing is any coupling between the two steps. processRedemption() is the Strategy
Delegate's transaction on the strategy manager, processRedemptions() is the redeemer's
transaction on the withdrawal manager, and neither checks that the request the cash
was raised for still exists.
uint256 assets_ = _convertToExitAssets(shares_, assetsUnderManagement());
require(assets_ != 0,
""MSM:PR:ZERO_ASSETS"");
require(assets_ <= availableToWithdraw(), ""MSM:PR:NOT_ENOUGH_LIQUIDITY"");
pendingSharesOf[poolManager_] -= shares_;
totalPendingShares
-= shares_;
sharesOf[poolManager_] -= shares_;
totalShares
-= shares_;
The shares are burned at the exit rate but the strategy's impairedAssets is unchanged, so
the difference stays in the strategy and is spread over every remaining share, most of
them the other pool's. That cross-pool transfer happens here, whatever the queue does
afterwards.
Between the two transactions the request owner can call removeSharesById(), which
requires only that the caller owns the request. The pool then holds cash it sold strategy
manager shares for at a discount, and the LP is still a depositor.
Consider the following example. Strategy AUM 1000 with 200 impaired, Pool A holds 600
shares and Pool B 400. Pool B redeems 100 shares to serve a request from an LP holding a
8

quarter of the pool:
assets = 100 * (1000 - 200) / 1000 = 80
20 stays in the strategy over the 900 remaining shares. At gross valuation Pool A is now 6
00 * 920 / 900 = 613.33 and Pool B is 300 * 920 / 900 + 80 = 386.67. Exit valuations
are unchanged, so nothing is visible while the impairment stands. The LP cancels and the
impairment is later removed:
• Pool A: 600, now 613.33.
• Pool B: 400, now 386.67, with nobody having exited. The LP who requested pays
3.33 of it, the other LPs pay 10.
Without the cancel the exiting LP is paid 80 for shares worth 100 at gross and carries the
whole 20. Only if the loss is realised at exactly the impaired amount is nobody worse off.
Impact
LPs who did not request a withdrawal pay, pro rata, for another LP's exit from an
impaired strategy once the impairment is removed. The other pool's gain is the same
with or without the cancel. Note: this requires the Strategy Delegate to process a
redemption while the strategy is impaired, since bots and the router are already locked
out while impairedAssets != 0.
