Issue M-1: V2 applies isTokenSupported on withdrawal
paths, so disabling a collateral permanently locks it
in the WHYPE/KHYPE market [RESOLVED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/67
Summary
DelphoRouterV2 validates every supplied token against the market's ConfigProvider on
three exit paths: requestWithdrawal, cancelWithdrawalRequest and repayAndWithdraw. De
lphoRouter gates only the entry path.
That asymmetry in V1 is deliberate and correct: disabling a collateral should stop new
deposits without orphaning existing positions. V2 breaks it. Once a collateral is disabled
in the WHYPE market, holders cannot withdraw it, cancel a pending request, or close
their position, and no V1 selector reaches that market's Vault to work around it.
Vulnerability Detail
V1 contains exactly one isTokenSupported call, inside _handleDeposit. Its withdrawal
functions perform no token validation, and neither does DelphoVault on requestWithdraw
al, cancelWithdrawalRequest or claimWithdrawal.
V2 adds the check to three exit paths.
withdrawCollateral, redeem and repayStable retain V1 parity, so the regression is
confined to these three.
_validateMarketToken reverts DR_UNSUPPORTED_TOKEN when config.isTokenSupported(toke
n) is false, and isTokenSupported returns the per-token enabled flag. So a routine updateT
oken(token, false, feed) immediately blocks all three paths for every holder of that
collateral.
Inherited V1 selectors resolve through delphoConfigProvider, which points at the stable
provider and therefore the stable Vault. A depositor in the WHYPE market has no
reachable path to that market's Vault other than the V2 overloads, so there is no
equivalent of the workaround that keeps the stable market's disabled collateral
accessible.
Impact
Any admin action that disables a WHYPE-market collateral, which is normal risk
management rather, permanently locks that collateral for every holder.
8

