Issue M-2: PSM mints and redeems USDV against
USDC at a hardcoded 1:1 with no oracle, so a USDC
depeg directly under-backs USDV [RESOLVED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/68
Summary
DelphoPSM.mint and DelphoPSM.redeem compute their output arithmetically from the
input amount, applying only a fee. Neither consults an oracle:
usdvOut = amount - fee;
// mint
collateralOut = usdvAmount - fee;
// redeem
The rest of the protocol prices the same USDC through Chainlink, and the ConfigProvider
already carries a stablecoin clamp for it. So the PSM is the only mint path in the system
that will accept depegged collateral at face value, up to psmMintCap of 2,000,000 USDV.
Vulnerability Detail
USDV is 6 decimals and USDC is 6 decimals, so amount - fee is a unit-for-unit swap at
exactly par regardless of market price. There is no price lookup, no deviation bound and
no circuit breaker in either direction.
The CDP path does the opposite. It values USDC through DelphoOracle.getUsdValuesStr
ict, and _getTokenPriceStrict applies the configured stablecoin clamp:
if (bounds.isStable && bounds.maxPrice != 0 && price > bounds.maxPrice) {
price = bounds.maxPrice;
}
On the live provider tokenPriceBounds(USDC) is (1000000, true), so the CDP already
treats USDC as at most one dollar while marking it down below par. The PSM ignores
both halves of that.
The result is direct over-issuance. If USDC trades at 0.95 dollars, anyone can buy it on the
open market and mint USDV from the PSM at par minus the 10 basis point mint fee. The
PSM's recorded liability, psmLiabilities[collateral], then exceeds the market value of
the collateral backing it by the depeg amount, and USDV becomes under-backed by
that difference across the whole minted volume. No attacker role, no privileged access
and no unusual sequencing is required: Router.psmMint is permissionless.
The exposure is bounded by psmMintCap, which the initializer sets to 2,000,000 USDV, and
by the mint fee. A one percent depeg with the cap fully used is roughly 20,000 dollars of
10

unbacked USDV. Redemption is exposed symmetrically: at 0.95 dollars a redeemer
receives a full unit of USDC per USDV, draining the liquid buffer faster in value terms than
the liability it retires.
Note that TokenPriceBounds carries only maxPrice and no floor, so the existing clamp
cannot express the redemption-side protection even if the PSM used the oracle today.
Impact
Any USDC depeg beyond the mint fee lets anyone convert discounted collateral into
full-value USDV, degrading USDV's backing for every holder up to the mint cap. Historic
USDC depegs have exceeded the 10 basis point mint fee by orders of magnitude, so the
fee is not a meaningful defence. The same asymmetry lets redeemers extract full-unit
collateral for depegged-value USDV, accelerating buffer depletion precisely when the
peg is under stress.
The protocol already owns the machinery to prevent this and applies it on the CDP path,
so this is an inconsistency between two mint paths for the same asset rather than a
missing capability.
