Issue L-3: DelphoRouterV2 re-implements six V1 func-
tions that are already reusable, four needing only a
visibility change and two needing nothing at all [RE-
SOLVED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/71
Summary
Every helper in DelphoRouter is declared private, which in Solidity is not accessible to a
derived contract. DelphoRouterV2 inherits DelphoRouter but consequently cannot call any
of them, so it re-implements four internal helpers. It additionally duplicates two external
self-call wrappers that were already inherited and callable exactly as they stand.
All of the V1 originals are market-agnostic: they take their dependencies as parameters
and reference no ConfigProvider. Changing private to internal on four of them, and
simply deleting the two redundant wrappers with no behavioural change.
Vulnerability Detail
There is no behavioural difference. The one apparent exception is not one: V1's if (usdvT
oMint == 0) return; is dead code, because the preceding if (debt >= targetDebt) ret
urn; already guarantees usdvToMint >= 1.
The four internal helpers are reusable because each receives everything it needs as an
argument. _deLeverageUser and _reLeverageUser take a DelphoContracts struct plus the
caps, _handleRemainingCollateralAfterRedemption takes the vault, and _validateLength
s is pure. None touches delphoConfigProvider or calls _configProvider(), so none is
bound to a single market.
The two external wrappers require no change at all. Both V1 wrappers are external and
therefore inherited, so this.deLeverageUserExternal is directly callable from V2, and the
msg.sender != address(this) guard is satisfied by V2's own self-call because both
resolve to the same proxy address. Neither wrapper is declared in IDelphoRouter or IDelp
hoRouterV2, so deleting the V2 copies changes no interface and no interfaceId.
_handleDeposit is the sole genuine exception and illustrates the pattern. It resolves the
provider internally with _configProvider(), hardcoding the default market, which is why
V2 needed a separate _handleMarketDeposit that accepts a provider argument. Unifying
that one requires a signature change, not just a visibility change.
21

Impact
Duplicated code.
