Issue L-4: PSM documented USDT but unsupported
[RESOLVED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/72
Summary
DelphoPSM documents USDT as a supported PSM collateral, but DelphoPSM::_isSupported
Collateral() resolves only StableCoinType.USDC from the config provider, so USDT can
never be minted against or deployed even though it is a fully configured stablecoinToken.
Vulnerability Detail
The contract level NatSpec states that supported collateral tokens are resolved via the
configured stablecoinToken mappings and names both USDC and USDT as the
supported set: DelphoPSM.sol#L13-L20.
The actual acceptance check reads a single entry of that mapping. DelphoPSM::_isSuppo
rtedCollateral() fetches cfg.stablecoinToken(StableCoinType.USDC) and returns true
only when the supplied address equals it: DelphoPSM.sol#L379-L386. The USDT branch
of the mapping is never consulted.
The config provider does populate USDT. DelphoHyperCoreConfig initializes stablecoinTok
en[StableCoinType.USDT] to a live token address alongside USDC, and exposes setStable
coinToken() so an admin can repoint either entry: DelphoHyperCoreConfig.sol#L48-L49.
The StableCoinType enum likewise carries a distinct USDT member:
DelphoExecutorTypes.sol#L26-L30.
Consequently DelphoPSM::mint() reverts with DPSM_UNSUPPORTED_COLLATERAL for USDT
(DelphoPSM.sol#L147) and DelphoPSM::deployReserves() reverts for the same reason (Del
phoPSM.sol#L253-L255). Note that DelphoPSM::redeem() never calls _isSupportedCollate
ral() at all, so the mint and redeem sides do not even agree on what ”supported” means.
Impact
The deployed PSM is single collateral in practice while its documentation, its config
surface and its enum all advertise two. Integrators and off chain tooling that trust the
NatSpec will build USDT mint flows that revert unconditionally. Because the accepted
asset is baked into an internal function rather than read from configuration, listing USDT
later requires a UUPS upgrade of DelphoPSM rather than an admin call to setStablecoinTo
ken(), which is a materially heavier and riskier operation than the configuration driven
design the comments describe. No funds are at risk, so this is Low.
23

