Issue L-5: PSM mint does not open redemption win-
dow [RESOLVED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/73
Summary
DelphoPSM::mint() credits psmLiabilities and globalBufferFunds but touches none of
the redemption window state that DelphoPSM::redeem() maintains, so the two legs of a
PSM round trip are accounted asymmetrically: minting creates redeemable liabilities
without opening, resetting or crediting any redemption window, while every redemption
permanently consumes the global per window budget.
Vulnerability Detail
DelphoPSM::redeem() is rate limited by two independent accumulators. The per user leg
reads psmStartRedeemableTimestamp[user][collateral], treats the accumulator as live
only while the elapsed time is under psmWindowDuration, and checks the running total
against psmMaxDailyRedemption: DelphoPSM.sol#L199-L207. The global leg is a tumbling
window keyed by psmGlobalRedemptionWindowStart[collateral] and accumulated into p
smGlobalDailyRedemptions[collateral] against psmMaxGlobalDailyRedemption:
DelphoPSM.sol#L209-L218. Both are written on the way out, and the user window start is
refreshed to block.timestamp on every single redemption: DelphoPSM.sol#L227-L230.
DelphoPSM::mint() updates only the books. It raises psmLiabilities[collateral] against
psmMintCap (DelphoPSM.sol#L159-L161) and splits the net amount into globalBufferFunds
and fundsForExecutor (DelphoPSM.sol#L163-L165). It never writes psmStartRedeemableTim
estamp, psmDailyRedemptions, psmGlobalRedemptionWindowStart or psmGlobalDailyRedempt
ions, and there is no mint side cap other than the cumulative psmMintCap:
DelphoPSM.sol#L137-L171.
Two concrete consequences follow.
First, a mint and redeem round trip is net destructive to redemption capacity. Fresh
collateral entering through DelphoPSM::mint() restores the buffer that a redemption
drained, but nothing gives back the psmGlobalDailyRedemptions[collateral] that the
redemption consumed. An address that deposits its own collateral and immediately
redeems the resulting USDV burns global budget that legitimate redeemers can no
longer use for the remainder of the window, at a cost of only the mint fee plus the
redeem fee, ten and twenty five basis points under the deployed defaults
(DelphoPSMConfig.sol#L19-L29). The per user cap does not stop this, since the
redemption accumulators are keyed by msg.sender forwarded from DelphoRouter::psmRe
deem() (DelphoRouter.sol#L215-L230) and any number of fresh addresses can be used.
Second, an honest user who has exhausted their per user allowance and then deposits
26

new collateral cannot get it back out. Their newly minted USDV is fully backed by the
liabilities and buffer they just created, but psmDailyRedemptions[user][collateral] is
untouched by the mint and psmStartRedeemableTimestamp[user][collateral] was
pushed forward to the timestamp of their last redemption, so the wait runs a full psmWind
owDuration from that redemption rather than from the start of any window the mint
might have opened.
Impact
Redemption capacity for a collateral can be exhausted for the rest of a window by an
attacker who supplies their own collateral, at a cost bounded by the combined mint and
redeem fees on the amount cycled, denying other USDV holders the PSM peg floor until p
smGlobalRedemptionWindowStart[collateral] rolls. No funds are lost and the attacker
pays fees to the protocol on every cycle, so the realistic outcome is temporary griefing of
the redemption path plus the accounting inconsistency that a user's own fresh deposit
does not extend their own redemption allowance. Low.
