Issue L-8: Redeem incentive for unhealthy markets
[ACKNOWLEDGED]
Source:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit2/2026-08-delpho-update-aug-27th-2026/issues/78
This issue has been acknowledged by the team but won't be fixed at this time.
Summary
DelphoStableManager::redeem() credits the redeemer collateral worth exactly the net
redeemed amount and charges a redemption fee on top of it, so a redemption is always
value negative for the caller at peg. Redemption is the only permissionless action that
deleverages a market, and it is the one action the protocol pays nobody to perform, so it
does not happen when the market needs it most.
Vulnerability Detail
DelphoStableManager::redeem() splits the request into a stability pool portion and a
fallback borrower portion, and both portions settle at a flat one to one USD exchange
with a fee deducted from the redeemer's side (redeem).
On the pool path, DelphoStableManager::_redeemFromPool() computes the fee off the
gross amount and reduces the seize budget by it (_redeemFromPool). The collateral
actually handed over is selected by StableManagerLib::selectCollateralProRata(),
which caps the seized USD value at the requested amount and never above it
(selectCollateralProRata). The gross amount is then burned from the redeemer while only
the net value of collateral is credited (burn and credit).
A redeemer burning 1 USDV therefore receives 0.995 USD of collateral, and that collateral
is not even liquid on arrival: DelphoRouterV2::redeem() pushes the credited collateral
through requestWithdrawal, so the portion beyond the vault buffer is queued to a future
epoch and the redeemer carries price risk on it (_requestRedeemedCollateral).
Meanwhile the same transaction is unambiguously good for the market. Debt and
collateral are both reduced by the same USD amount, so for any market whose LTV is
above zero the ratio falls. A market holding 100,000 of collateral against 90,000 of debt
sits at 90% LTV; a redemption of 30,000 leaves 70,000 of collateral against 60,000 of
debt, which is 85.7% LTV, further from the liquidation threshold checked in VaultMath::va
lidateLiquidation() (validateLiquidation) and from the borrow ceiling in VaultMath::val
idateLtv() (validateLtv). The same holds per position, and DelphoStableManager::_redee
mFromBorrowers() already directs the pressure at the weakest borrowers by walking the
sorted debt list from its head, which is the lowest LCR node (snapshot from getHead).
35

Impact
No direct loss of funds. The consequence is that the protocol's only permissionless
deleveraging valve is dormant by construction. Deleveraging depends entirely on an
external USDV discount that exceeds 0.5% plus gas, which is precisely the situation the
deleveraging was meant to prevent. In the window where a market climbs from its target
LTV toward liquidationThresholdBps, redemption would restore health cheaply and
nothing pulls it in, so the market instead reaches the point where losses are realised
through liquidation, bad debt accounting in DelphoStableManager and fallback debt
socialisation across the remaining borrowers.
