Source: https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-09-maple-sept-7th-2026/issues/50
Summary
MapleStrategyManager::convertToExitShares() returns how many shares are required to
exit a given number of assets, rounding down. processRedemption() converts the sent
shares by calling _convertToExitAssets(), which rounds down again, so the redemption
pays out less than the input assets initially sent to MapleStrategyManager::convertToExit
Shares().
Vulnerability Detail
As an example, with totalShares equal to 1_000_000e6 and exit assets of 1_500_000e6,
converting 100 USDC gives:
100e6 * 1_000_000e6 / 1_500_000e6 = 66_666_666
and redeeming 66_666_666 shares pays 1 less than the initial 100e6 USDC:
66_666_666 * 1_500_000e6 / 1_000_000e6 = 99_999_999
Impact
Bots calling MapleStrategyManager::convertToExitShares() expecting to get the shares
required to redeem the input assets will get less assets when the redemption goes
through.
