Section 2: Trust Assumptions
• Strategy Delegates are trusted, like Pool Delegates. A delegate sets its own fee
rate and can fund and operate its strategy even while it is impaired. The
Operational Admin is trusted to reassign a delegate only for an agreed handover.
• Liquidity bots and the Liquidity Router are trusted, Maple-operated infrastructure.
Caps limit how much capital they move, not when. Strategy Manager redemptions
have no queue and are assumed to be processed in the order they were requested.
• Managed wallets are trusted, Maple-operated addresses. Capital deployed to a
wallet leaves on-chain control, and the yield a wallet reports through realizeYield()
is taken as given, within yieldCap and the hourly cooldown.
• Impairments, loss recognition, yield cancellation and Sky's fallbackTout are
discretionary operator inputs, used without proof or delay. They directly set the
price at which pools exit a strategy.
• Aave, Sky's LitePSM and sUSDS are assumed to account correctly. Positions are
valued from their live balances and conversion rates, with no independent oracle.
• Allocation yield is released into AUM linearly, so it goes to whoever holds Strategy
Manager shares while it vests, not to whoever held them when it was earned. Bots
are assumed to avoid funding or redeeming pools on a shared Strategy Manager
while a large amount of yield is still vesting.
• Strategy Managers and Pool Managers are assumed to be created only through the
Strategy Deployer and Pool Deployer, with a non-zero bootstrap set in Globals. The
upgrade procedure is assumed to drain the legacy Aave and Sky strategies before
each pool is migrated.
