"
CB-223,FR-378,The bridged token reinitializer function lacks access control,Trail_of_Bits__2026-09-gensyn-token-minting-cap-securityreview.pdf,Trail of Bits,gensyn,2026-08-07,no,no,2026-09,Informational,"Detailed Findings 
 
1. The bridged token reinitializer function lacks access control 
Severity: Informational 
Difficulty: Not Applicable 
Type: Access Controls 
Finding ID: TOB-GEN-CAP-1 
Target: token/src/BridgedGensynToken.sol 
 
Description 
The reinitializer function, which destroys the bridged token’s initial supply, carries no 
caller authorization, while the two supply-changing functions beside it are both role-gated. 
function reinitialize(address _recipient) external reinitializer(2) { 
    // Burn initial supply from recipient 
    _burn(_recipient, INITIAL_SUPPLY); 
 
    // Ensure total supply is zero after upgrade 
    /// @dev This is the bridged token, so its supply should start at 0 
    assert(totalSupply() == 0); 
} 
 
/// @inheritdoc IMintableBurnable 
function mint(address _to, uint256 _amount) external onlyRole(MINTER_ROLE) returns 
(bool success) { 
    _mint(_to, _amount); 
    success = true; 
} 
Figure 1.1: The reinitializer function accepts any caller and any recipient, unlike the 
role-gated mint function beside it. (token/src/BridgedGensynToken.sol#L28–L41) 
OpenZeppelin’s reinitializer modifier enforces only the initialization version and 
performs no caller-authorization check. Consequently, any account can call reinitialize on a 
proxy that has not yet consumed version two. The call succeeds only if _recipient holds 
INITIAL_SUPPLY, and burning that amount reduces totalSupply() to zero; otherwise, 
_burn or the trailing assertion reverts and rolls back the version change. In the deployment 
produced by the bridged-token script, initialize assigns the entire initial supply to 
0x000…dEaD. An attacker can therefore successfully call reinitialize only by specifying 
that address, which produces the same zero-supply state intended by the deployer. The 
defect becomes exploitable if a future upgrade points a compatible proxy at this 
​
        Trail of Bits​
10​
Gensyn Token Minting Cap​
        PUBLIC​
​
Security Assessment 

implementation, while version two remains unused and the entire initial supply is held by a 
live address: any account could specify that address and irreversibly burn its tokens. 
Exploit Scenario 
Gensyn adds a bridge minting and burning capability to an existing canonical GensynToken 
proxy by upgrading it to BridgedGensynToken, a step the deployment plan does not 
describe but the contract hierarchy permits and the timelock can authorize. That proxy 
minted its full supply to a live treasury and never consumed version two, so the upgrade 
alone arms reinitialize against a live balance. Eve observes it and calls reinitialize 
in the same block, naming the treasury as the recipient; the version check passes, the burn 
succeeds because the treasury holds the entire supply, and the assertion passes because 
the supply is now zero. No role gates the call, and the timelock that authorized the upgrade 
does not govern it, so one unreviewed operational step places the whole supply within 
reach of any account. 
Recommendations 
Short term, restrict reinitialize to DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE and update the deployment 
script to match, since it calls the function from the broadcasting account while that role is 
granted to the timelock. This will address the issue because the burn can then occur only at 
the direction of the account the token recognizes as its administrator. 
Long term, fold the supply reduction into initialize so that no second initialization 
version is ever exposed on a live proxy. This will prevent the issue from recurring because 
an unconsumed initializer version will stop being a standing capability that any future 
implementation upgrade can arm. 
 
 
 
