2. The bridged token deployment script hard-codes one Safe address as the 
sole timelock proposer on every chain 
Severity: Low 
Difficulty: High 
Type: Configuration 
Finding ID: TOB-GEN-CAP-2 
Target: token/script/BridgedGensynToken.s.sol 
 
Description 
The bridged token deployment script has no chain ID validation and names a hard-coded 
address as the timelock’s sole proposer and canceller; it does not set a timelock admin. It 
therefore produces identical governance wiring wherever it runs, unlike the canonical 
deployment script, which restricts itself to two Gensyn chain IDs. 
Because TimelockController grants DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE only to itself when the 
admin argument is zero, deploying to a chain where that address cannot originate 
transactions yields a timelock whose proposer set cannot be repaired through its own role 
machinery: MINTER_ROLE and BURNER_ROLE never reach the bridge adapter, supply stays 
frozen at zero, and _authorizeUpgrade is unsatisfiable until the entire stack is 
redeployed. 
That address is a three-of-five Safe address on the Ethereum mainnet holding neither 
PROPOSER_ROLE nor CANCELLER_ROLE on the live bridged timelock, and it has no code at 
all on Base, Arbitrum One, Optimism, or BSC. Therefore, the constant is not merely 
chain-dependent but also stale: production has already rotated governance away from it, 
so the next run of this script names a retired proposer on Ethereum as readily as on a 
chain the token has never reached. 
uint256 timelockMinDelay = 7 days; 
 
address gensynSafeAddress = 0x90442673dae1b1572a3D994A4D795c8977A97ECD; 
 
address[] memory timelockProposers = new address[](1); 
timelockProposers[0] = gensynSafeAddress; 
 
address[] memory timelockExecutors = new address[](1); 
timelockExecutors[0] = address(0); // allow anyone to execute 
 
address timelockAdmin = address(0); // renounce admin role to prevent centralization 
 
address gensynTokenRecipient = 0x000000000000000000000000000000000000dEaD; 
 
​
        Trail of Bits​
