1. Forged storage values can pass state proof verification 
Severity: Informational 
Difficulty: Low 
Type: Data Validation 
Finding ID: TOB-SCRLM-1 
Target: src/libraries/verifier/PatriciaMerkleTrieVerifier.sol, 
src/L1/rollup/ScrollChainCommitmentVerifier.sol 
 
Description 
PatriciaMerkleTrieVerifier can accept a fabricated storage value as genuine. The 
verifier loses the expected hash of the proof node containing that value, allowing 
verifyStateCommitment to return an attacker-chosen value under a genuine finalized 
state root. 
verifyStateCommitment accepts account and storage proofs from the caller and returns 
the proven storage value. The account proof provides storageHash, the expected root of 
the account’s storage tree. For the storage proof, walkTree reconstructs this root as 
rootHash and returns the hash expected for the final node. hashCompareSelect hashes 
the caller-supplied final node and extracts its storage value. A valid proof must compare 
rootHash with storageHash and the supplied node’s hash with its expected hash. 
ptr, rootHash, hash, path := walkTree(key, ptr) 
 
// ... 
ptr, hash, encodedPath, storageValue, vlen := hashCompareSelect(ptr, 2, 0, 1) 
// ... 
switch rootHash 
case 0 { 
    // ... 
    require(eq(hash, storageHash), ""Storage root mismatch"") 
} 
default { 
    // ... 
    require(eq(rootHash, storageHash), ""Storage root mismatch"") 
} 
Figure 1.1: Storage-proof decoding and root validation for single-node and multi-node proofs 
(scroll-contracts-internal/src/libraries/verifier/PatriciaMerkleTrieVerif
ier.sol#L482–L500) 
​
        Trail of Bits​
12​
Scroll Foundation TVL to Ethereum​
        PUBLIC​
​
Security Assessment 

However, both final-node hashes use the same hash variable. hashCompareSelect 
overwrites the expected hash returned by walkTree with the caller-supplied node’s hash, 
so they are never compared. For multi-node proofs, the default branch checks only 
rootHash, allowing the caller to keep the genuine proof but replace its final value. The 
single-node case is unaffected because case 0 checks the final-node hash directly. 
A consumer could therefore accept fabricated L2 state. We found no public deployment or 
consumer of this verifier version, so we report the issue as informational. 
Exploit Scenario 
An application relies on verifyStateCommitment before releasing assets. An attacker 
copies a genuine multi-node storage proof and changes the value in its final node. The 
genuine nodes reproduce the finalized state root, while the unchecked final node supplies 
the attacker’s value. The application then releases assets based on nonexistent L2 state. 
Recommendations 
Preserve the expected final-node hash returned by walkTree and require it to match the 
submitted node’s hash for multi-node proofs. Test valid and modified multi-node proofs 
and the single-node case.
 
