2. Key rotation can lock failed encrypted deposits 
Severity: Medium 
Difficulty: Low 
Type: Timing 
Finding ID: TOB-SCRLM-2 
Target: src/validium/L1ERC20GatewayValidium.sol, 
src/validium/L1ScrollMessengerValidium.sol, 
src/validium/ScrollChainValidiumV2.sol 
 
Description 
In the validium deposit flow, the replayMessage function re-enqueues the original 
ciphertext at a new queue index, while l2geth selects the encryption key from that new 
index. If the key rotates between a failed deposit and its replay, the decryption attempt 
returns the ErrDecryptionFailed error. l2geth then includes the encrypted call 
unchanged, causing the L2 gateway call to revert before minting and leaving the deposited 
tokens locked in L1 escrow. 
Validium deposits encrypt only the L2 recipient. The sequencer decrypts the recipient 
before executing the deposit on L2 (design). When a deposit fails on L2 (e.g., due to 
insufficient gas), a user can call replayMessage with a higher gas limit. The function 
rebuilds the previously enqueued cross-domain calldata, including the ciphertext, and 
appends it at the queue’s next index. 
bytes memory _xDomainCalldata = _encodeXDomainCalldata(_from, _to, _value, 
_messageNonce, _message); 
bytes32 _xDomainCalldataHash = keccak256(_xDomainCalldata); 
 
// ... 
uint256 _nextQueueIndex = 
IL1MessageQueueV2(messageQueueV2).nextCrossDomainMessageIndex(); 
IL1MessageQueueV2(messageQueueV2).appendCrossDomainMessage(counterpart, 
_newGasLimit, _xDomainCalldata); 
Figure 2.1: replayMessage re-enqueues previously enqueued calldata at the next queue index. 
(scroll-contracts-internal/src/L1/L1ScrollMessenger.sol#L234–L253) 
Registering a new key makes it apply from the queue’s next index (rotation). l2geth then 
selects the key for each message from its current queue index. 
