Figure 2.2: l2geth selects the replay's key from its queue index and retains the encrypted call 
after decryption fails. (go-ethereum/miner/scroll_worker.go#L868–L895) 
When decryption succeeds, l2geth replaces the finalizeDepositERC20Encrypted 
function call with a call to the gateway’s finalizeDepositERC20 function. After a 
rotation, decrypting the old ciphertext with the new key returns the 
ErrDecryptionFailed error (decryption). l2geth then leaves the queued 
relayMessage function call unchanged, so the L2ScrollMessenger contract forwards 
the finalizeDepositERC20Encrypted function call to the L2 gateway. The gateway does 
not implement this function, so the call reverts before minting. The messenger emits the 
FailedRelayedMessage event without marking the message as executed, while the relay 
transaction succeeds (gateway interface, relay handling). 
The replayMessage function requires a previously enqueued message hash, so the user 
cannot replace its ciphertext with one encrypted under the new key. Replays under that key 
follow the same failure path. 
Exploit Scenario 
A user's deposit encrypted under K0 runs out of gas on L2. Before the user replays it with a 
higher gas limit, the key manager rotates to K1. The replay retains the K0 ciphertext but 
receives a K1 queue index, so authenticated decryption fails. l2geth includes the 
encrypted call unchanged; the gateway call fails before minting, while the outer transaction 
succeeds and the deposited tokens remain escrowed on L1. 
Recommendations 
Short term, bind each encrypted message and its replays to the key used for the original 
message, regardless of their queue indices, and use that binding for sequencer decryption, 
bundle construction, and proof verification. This keeps key selection consistent with the 
retained ciphertext. 
