An attacker can stop a valid account proof early and end it with an empty leaf, falsely 
claiming that an existing account is absent. The retained portion still produces the finalized 
state root, and the missing leaf check causes the verifier to treat the account’s storage root 
as zero. An empty storage proof can then return zero for the requested slot. 
We did not identify any current consumer of the affected verifier. The issue is reported as 
informational because a future consumer could accept false zero values. 
Exploit Scenario 
An external application uses verifyStateCommitment to check a storage slot in a 
historical pre-Euclid batch. An attacker submits authentic account-branch nodes truncated 
at a nonzero child, followed by empty account and storage nodes. The computed state root 
matches the finalized root, and the application accepts zero for a slot in an existing 
account. 
Recommendations 
Short term, identify consumers of pre-Euclid proofs and have them reject proofs that use 
an empty account leaf until the verifier is fixed. 
Long term, require leafHash == 0 for an empty account leaf, matching the storage-proof 
check. Test that empty-account proofs succeed for an empty root and a zero selected child, 
and reject proofs that replace a nonzero selected child with an empty leaf immediately 
below the root or deeper in the trie. 
 
 
 
