Description 
Under the planned validium design, l2geth and the prover parse encrypted deposit 
recipients differently. An unprivileged depositor can exploit this mismatch to make l2geth 
build a block for which the prover cannot produce a valid proof. The affected block and 
blocks built on it cannot finalize until operators replace the block with one the prover 
accepts or align the implementations. 
Users call the depositERC20 function to bridge ERC-20 tokens from Ethereum to an L2 
address that remains encrypted until the deposit is executed. l2geth decrypts the address 
when building the L2 block. A prover must then validate the block before Ethereum 
finalizes it. 
depositERC20 accepts the encrypted L2 address as the _to byte array. The gateway 
copies _to into the queued L1-to-L2 message without validating its key encoding. 
bytes memory _message = abi.encodeCall( 
    IL2ERC20GatewayValidium.finalizeDepositERC20Encrypted, 
    (_token, _l2Token, _from, _to, _amount, _l2Data) 
); 
IL1ScrollMessenger(messenger).sendMessage{value: msg.value}(counterpart, 0, 
_message, _gasLimit, _from); 
Figure 6.1: The gateway copies the unvalidated _to bytes into the queued message. 
(L1ERC20GatewayValidium.sol#L256–L262) 
The _to bytes start with an encoded 65-byte public key. The expected uncompressed 
format requires the first byte to be 0x04; the remaining 64 bytes contain the key data. 
When l2geth processes the queued deposit, it decrypts _to using the Go parser below. 
The parser ignores the prefix byte and constructs the key from only the remaining 64 bytes. 
