(ecies-go/ecies.go#L65–L70) 
After l2geth builds the block, the prover reprocesses the original queued transaction to 
check that the block contains the correct deposit. It decodes the original _to value and 
calls secret_key.try_decrypt. This enters the Rust decryption path below. 
try_decrypt calls split_ciphertext, which passes all 65 public-key bytes, including the 
prefix, to PublicKey::try_from_bytes. 
pub fn try_decrypt<'a>(&self, ciphertext: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, DecryptError> { 
    let (ephemeral_pk, nonce, tag, buffer) = split_ciphertext(ciphertext)?; 
    // ... 
} 
 
// ... 
 
fn split_ciphertext(ciphertext: &[u8]) -> Result<(PublicKey, Nonce, Tag, &[u8]), 
DecryptError> { 
    // ... 
    let (ephemeral_pk_bytes, remaining) = 
        ciphertext.split_at(UNCOMPRESSED_PUBLIC_KEY_SIZE); 
    let ephemeral_pk = PublicKey::try_from_bytes(ephemeral_pk_bytes)?; 
    // ... 
} 
 
// ... 
 
pub fn try_from_bytes(input: impl AsRef<[u8]>) -> Result<Self, KeyError> { 
    EncodedPoint::<Secp256k1>::from_bytes(input) 
        .ok() 
        .and_then(|point| { 
            
elliptic_curve::PublicKey::<Secp256k1>::from_encoded_point(&point).into_option() 
        }) 
        .map(PublicKey) 
        .ok_or_else(|| KeyError) 
} 
Figure 6.3: The prover's Rust decryption path validates the complete 65-byte public key. 
(decryption, size definition, key extraction, validation) 
EncodedPoint::from_bytes accepts a 65-byte public key only when its prefix identifies 
the uncompressed format (0x04). Any other prefix makes try_from_bytes return 
KeyError, which propagates through try_decrypt to validium::decrypt. 
