Security Assessment 

The prover then checks whether l2geth placed the correct deposit transaction in the 
block. validium_tx is the original queued transaction, while tx_in_block is the L2 
transaction included by l2geth. If Rust decrypts the recipient, the prover expects 
tx_in_block to contain the decrypted deposit. If Rust rejects the recipient, the prover 
expects l2geth to have left the original transaction unchanged. 
match validium::decrypt(validium_tx, secret_key) { 
    Ok(decrypted) => { 
        assert_eq!(decrypted, *tx_in_block); 
    } 
    Err(_) => { 
        assert_eq!(*validium_tx, *tx_in_block); 
    } 
} 
Figure 6.4: The prover compares its expected transaction with the transaction included by 
l2geth (zkvm-prover/crates/types/chunk/src/scroll/types.rs#L238–L250) 
An attacker can force the two implementations to disagree by creating a valid encrypted 
recipient and replacing its 0x04 prefix byte with 0x05. l2geth’s Go parser ignores the 
changed byte, constructs the same key, and includes the decrypted deposit. The prover’s 
Rust parser rejects the modified encoding, so validium::decrypt returns an error and 
the prover expects the original encrypted transaction. Because tx_in_block contains the 
decrypted deposit, the equality check fails, and this prover cannot produce the proof 
finalizeBundle requires to finalize the block. The same applies to any prefix that Rust 
rejects but Go ignores. 
Without that proof, finalization cannot advance past the affected block, so later blocks and 
their withdrawals remain unfinalized. Removing the affected block and rebuilding from its 
parent is not enough: unchanged l2geth reads the next queue index from that parent and 
processes the same queued deposit again. Recovery requires either changing the proving 
system to accept the existing block or patching l2geth before rebuilding it. 
Any user can trigger the issue by bridging one unit of any standard ERC-20 they hold from 
their L1 account to their own L2 address. 
Exploit Scenario 
An attacker encrypts their own L2 address, replaces the required 0x04 prefix byte with 
0x05, and bridges one unit of any standard ERC-20 they hold. l2geth ignores the prefix 
byte and includes the decrypted deposit in an L2 block. The prover rejects the encoding 
and expects the original encrypted transaction, so it cannot produce the proof required to 
finalize the block. Rewinding the block without patching l2geth only recreates the same 
mismatch. 
