Security Assessment 

Figure 7.1: A zero Governor argument writes the supplied types and leaves the guard open. 
(agora-governor/src/ProposalTypesConfigurator.sol#L44–L56) 
However, because the guard checks only the stored address, a call with address(0) writes 
the caller’s proposal types and leaves the guard open. When the Governor is deployed 
afterward, its initializer calls initialize again and succeeds. It overwrites types zero 
through six with Scroll’s settings but leaves any type at index seven or above in place. 
Step 04 sets the Governor’s proposal threshold in a later transaction than its deployment, 
so in between, any account can create a proposal under the planted type. Such a proposal 
succeeds without any votes if its type has no approval threshold (figure 7.2), and because 
L1 SCR is not minted until step 14, no holder can vote against it. 
uint256 approvalThreshold = 
PROPOSAL_TYPES_CONFIGURATOR.proposalTypes(proposal.proposalType).approvalThreshold; 
 
if (approvalThreshold == 0) return true; 
Figure 7.2: A zero approval threshold succeeds. 
(agora-governor/src/AgoraGovernor.sol#L202–L204) 
The step 04 post-check detects the extra type and blocks, but threat T2 and the runbook 
incorrectly state that a front-run makes step 04 revert, so they describe no explicit recovery 
procedure for the case described in this finding. If the operator clears the type instead of 
redeploying, which is the only change the post-check accepts, the attacker’s proposal can 
execute through the L1 Timelock after step 21 unless the admin Safe cancels it. 
Exploit Scenario 
During step 04, Mallory calls initialize(address(0), types) with an extra type seven 
that has a zero quorum and approval threshold. The Governor deploys successfully, and 
before setProposalThreshold executes, Mallory proposes a transfer of Timelock-held 
assets under type seven. The post-check reports the extra type, and the operator clears it 
and continues with the same Governor. Mallory’s proposal succeeds with no votes. After 
step 21, she queues it and executes it once the delay passes. 
Recommendations 
Treat any unexpected configurator state at step 04 as a failed deployment that requires 
redeploying the configurator and the Governor, correct T2 and the runbook, and cancel any 
unexpected proposal before step 21. 
 
 
 
