9. Validium migrate function does not require paused deposit paths 
Severity: Low 
Difficulty: High 
Type: Data Validation 
Finding ID: TOB-SCRLM-9 
Target: 
scroll-contracts-internal/src/validium/ScrollChainValidiumV2.sol, 
sunset-migration/validium-migration/docs/DESIGN.md 
 
Description 
The migrate function of the ScrollChainValidiumV2 contract, which converts Scroll 
from a rollup into a validium, does not verify that the L1 deposit paths are paused. If the 
activation operations execute out of order, the chain enters validium mode while the 
existing messenger and gateways still accept plaintext deposits, which violates the threat 
model’s V4 invariant. 
The activation runs through Timelock operations that the admin Safe schedules, and the 
executor Safe executes at a time of its choosing. Operation 1 pauses L1MessageQueueV2, 
the queue to which every L1-to-L2 message is appended, and EnforcedTxGateway, which 
lets L1 users force transactions onto L2. After the sequencer includes every queued 
message, operation 2 upgrades ScrollChain and calls migrate (figure 9.1). Both 
operations are scheduled together on day 0, and nothing on-chain or in the Timelock 
schedule requires operation 1 to execute first. 
| 1 | Operation 1 | Pause the queue. Pause `EnforcedTxGateway`; it must stay paused 
([V4](THREAT_MODEL.md#core-invariants)). Raise both enforced-batch delays to the 
maximum (about 194 days) | 
| 1 | Off-chain | The sequencer includes every queued message and halts at block X. 
The prover finalizes through X | 
| 1 | Operation 2, one batch | Pause `ScrollChain`, `upgradeAndCall(migrate(key, 
keyManager))`, register the validium verifier at start index 0, unpause. Restart 
from X+1 in validium mode | 
| 2 | Weeks | Run with deposits closed. A withdrawal after the last rollup bundle 
can claim once the first validium bundle finalizes. Failed deposits replay only 
after phase 3. Exit when real bundles finalize, a canary withdrawal claims on L1, 
and a deposit to the live key decrypts on a devnet | 
Figure 9.1: The activation steps that close deposits and then upgrade ScrollChain. 
(sunset-migration/validium-migration/docs/DESIGN.md#L20–L23) 
The migrate function is meant to run only when the chain is ready for the switch. It checks 
that the chain is paused, that every batch is finalized, that enforced mode is off, that every 
V1 message is finalized, and that the V2 queue is drained (figure 9.2). 
