Figure 9.2: The migrate precondition checks 
(scroll-contracts-internal/src/validium/ScrollChainValidiumV2.sol#L107–L1
22) 
However, migrate checks only that the queue is empty, not that it is paused, and it does 
not check EnforcedTxGateway at all. The design relies on the drain check to reject an 
early operation 2 (figure 9.3). 
**Retry.** If operation 2 runs before the drain, `migrate` reverts. The 
operation stays scheduled; run it again after the drain. 
Figure 9.3: The design assumes that an early operation 2 reverts. 
(sunset-migration/validium-migration/docs/DESIGN.md#L26–L27) 
This assumption holds only while messages are pending. The queue is empty whenever the 
sequencer has included every L1 message and the prover has finalized it. In that state, 
migrate succeeds even though operation 1 has not been executed. 
Exploit Scenario 
The admin Safe schedules operations 1 and 2 on day 0. After the delay, the executor Safe 
executes operation 2 before operation 1 while the queue happens to be drained. The 
migrate call succeeds, and the sequencer restarts in validium mode. Alice deposits ETH 
through the existing L1GatewayRouter; l2geth includes her plaintext deposit, and her 
funds enter the validium during the canary period in which deposits should be closed. 
Recommendations 
Short term, make migrate revert unless the messageQueuePaused function of the 
L1MessageQueueV2 contract returns true and the queue’s EnforcedTxGateway is 
paused. This ensures that operation 2 succeeds only while operation 1 is in effect. 
