Figure 14.2: The canary claim waits for the message to be executed. 
(sunset-migration/runner-core/src/steps/canary.ts#L75–L82) 
However, the step never inspects the receipt for FailedRelayedMessage, so a failed relay 
looks the same as a pending one. Because the step record already holds a transaction 
hash, every rerun only re-observes that receipt and never prepares a new relay, even after 
the gateway fault is fixed. 
Exploit Scenario 
The operator runs the canary claim step after step 20. The L1 gateway rejects the canary 
withdrawal because of a configuration fault, and the relay transaction succeeds with a 
FailedRelayedMessage event. The runner reports that it is waiting for execution, and the 
operator reruns the step for hours with the same result while the gateway fault goes 
undiagnosed. 
Recommendations 
Short term, make the canary claim step report blocked when the relay receipt contains 
FailedRelayedMessage, and document how to retry the relay. 
Long term, allow steps that wait for an external state change to prepare a new attempt 
when a mined attempt did not produce that change.
 
