Description
During the native value withdrawal process, the recipients are called in a loop, where each
recipient receives a call:
function _settleNativeWithdrawals(
BridgeLane lane,
address asset,
uint64 nonce,
WithdrawalRecord[] calldata withdrawals,
WithdrawalRecord[] memory settledWithdrawals
) private returns (uint256 settledCount) {
// [...]
for (uint256 index = 0; index < withdrawals.length; ++index) {
WithdrawalRecord calldata withdrawal = withdrawals[index];
(bool succeeded,) = withdrawal.recipient.call{value:
withdrawal.amount}("""");
if (succeeded) {
// [...]
This low-level call does not specify a gas limit, so the maximum possible gas (63/64 of the
remaining gas) is forwarded into the call. The recipient contract can therefore consume as much
of that gas as it is able to.
Because only 1/64 of the pre-call gas remains afterward, the execution context is unlikely to have
enough gas left to run the remainder of the logic.
Impact
A malicious recipient can block other recipients from receiving their funds by consuming all the
gas.
It is also theoretically possible for a malicious recipient to collude with the block builder to make
the sender of the withdrawal transaction spend more gas. This increases the value of the
transaction to the block builder, and part of that value can be captured back to the recipient,
