uint256 amount) external onlyOwner nonReentrant {
// [...]
if (_bridgeStorage().everSupported[asset])
revert SupportedAssetRecovery(asset);
IERC20(asset).safeTransfer(recipient, amount);
emit UnsupportedTokenRecovered(asset, recipient, amount);
}
As the comment notes, the everSupported check prevents this recovery function from moving
funds held for an asset that the bridge has ever enabled. The function is also onlyOwner, so only
the owner can recover assets.
However, the contract is upgradable, and upgrade authorization is onlyOwner as well:
function _authorizeUpgrade(address) internal override onlyOwner {}
This means that the owner has effective full control over all the assets owned by this contract,
because they can always upgrade the contract to an implementation that transfers the assets.
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 13 of 24

EVM Deposit/Withdraw Bridge Smart Contract Security Assessment
August 18, 2026
Impact
The everSupported guard overstates what the contract guarantees. An owner can still transfer
assets that the bridge has previously enabled, either through a contract upgrade that clears the
everSupported flag or through a contract upgrade that transfers the assets directly.
Recommendations
We recommend adding a lower-privileged role so that the account that assists with token
recovery can be meaningfully restricted by the everSupported flag.
Alternatively, with no code changes, we recommend documenting that the everSupported flag is
only a guardrail and not a security guarantee, since the owner is permitted to upgrade the contract.
Note that it is not feasible to determine, during a contract upgrade, whether a future version of the
contract would respect the same invariant.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Pacifica, and a fix was implemented in commit 2c3338d1 ↗.
