3.3.
Withdrawer gas limits may cause incorrect withdrawal failures
Target
PcfaBridge.sol
Category
Coding Mistakes
Severity
Informational
Likelihood
N/A
Impact
Informational
Description
During the token value withdrawal process, the contract calls itself using a try-catch in order to
catch reverts during the withdrawal process:
function _settleTokenWithdrawals(
// [...]
) private returns (uint256 settledCount) {
for (uint256 index = 0; index < withdrawals.length; ++index) {
WithdrawalRecord calldata withdrawal = withdrawals[index];
try this.withdrawTokenRecord(asset, withdrawal.recipient,
withdrawal.amount) {
// [...]
} catch (bytes memory reason) {
_emitWithdrawalFailure(lane, asset, nonce, index, withdrawal,
reason);
}
}
}
The withdrawTokenRecord function then makes another call to transfer the token:
function withdrawTokenRecord(address asset, address recipient, uint256 amount)
external {
if (msg.sender != address(this)) revert UnauthorizedSelfCall();
// [...]
token.safeTransfer(recipient, amount);
// [...]
}
The intent of the try-catch is to allow other withdrawals to proceed even if one of them fails due to
a downstream issue with the specific token implementation contract.
However, the withdrawTokenRecord function may revert through no fault of the token
implementation contract if it was not given enough gas to complete. The amount of gas available
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 15 of 24

EVM Deposit/Withdraw Bridge Smart Contract Security Assessment
August 18, 2026
to it is ultimately set by the caller of batchWithdraw or spotBatchWithdraw.
Impact
Note that the sender of the batchWithdraw or spotBatchWithdraw functions is a permissioned
withdrawer with a role, so they are broadly trusted to withdraw assets properly.
However, if they call those functions with a carefully limited quantity of gas, then it is possible for
them to force a revert inside the child execution context. Depending on optimization parameters
and gas costs, there may be enough gas left over to complete the parent context. If it does
complete, then the sender will have manufactured a withdrawal failure.
Recommendations
We recommend measuring the amount of gas left before the call to withdrawTokenRecord in order
to enforce a gas minimum. If the call succeeds, then the gas does not need to be checked.
However, if the call fails and the minimum was violated, then it should be treated as a revert of the
entire call to batchWithdraw or spotBatchWithdraw rather than as a single withdrawal failure.
Note that although the gas does not strictly need to be checked if the call succeeds, it may be
more friendly to gas estimation algorithms to check the gas left unconditionally.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Pacifica, and a fix was implemented in commit 2c3338d1 ↗.
