determined by hashing a concatenation of a hash of the mapping key (in this case, the asset) with
the slot. This means that the asset needs to be hashed multiple times in order to access each of
these fields.
Most actions, however, use multiple fields. For instance, a single call to deposit accesses the
balanceAssets field to determine whether the asset is depositEnabled and then accesses the
balanceDepositNonces field to read and set the relevant nonce.
So, the asset is being hashed multiple times with this storage layout.
Impact
This storage layout causes unnecessary hashing, due to the repetitive asset key.
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 17 of 24

EVM Deposit/Withdraw Bridge Smart Contract Security Assessment
August 18, 2026
Recommendations
We recommend adding a single AssetData struct definition and storing all of the current fields in a
single mapping keyed by asset address:
struct AssetData {
AssetStatus balanceAsset;
AssetStatus spotAsset;
bool everSupported;
uint64 balanceWithdrawNonce;
uint64 spotWithdrawNonce;
uint64 balanceDepositNonce;
uint64 spotDepositNonce;
}
struct BridgeStorage {
mapping(address asset => AssetData data) assetData;
uint64 maxBatchSize;
bool withdrawalsPaused;
}
With this or a similar storage layout, each asset address would need to be hashed only once in
order to obtain the AssetData storage pointer, and all subsequent storage reads and writes could
be based on fixed offsets from that pointer.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Pacifica, and a fix was implemented in commit 2c3338d1 ↗.
