4.1.
A single withdrawal nonce across multiple withdrawers could result in coor-
dination hazards
Every EVM account that holds the withdrawer role for a specific lane-asset pair can call the
batchWithdraw and spotBatchWithdraw functions to withdraw assets:
/// @inheritdoc IPcfaBridge
function batchWithdraw(address asset, uint64 nonce, WithdrawalRecord[]
calldata withdrawals)
external
nonReentrant
whenNotPaused
{
_checkRole(withdrawerRole(BridgeLane.Balance, asset), msg.sender);
_batchWithdraw(BridgeLane.Balance, asset, nonce, withdrawals);
}
/// @inheritdoc IPcfaBridge
function spotBatchWithdraw(address asset, uint64 nonce, WithdrawalRecord[]
calldata withdrawals)
external
nonReentrant
whenNotPaused
{
_checkRole(withdrawerRole(BridgeLane.Spot, asset), msg.sender);
_batchWithdraw(BridgeLane.Spot, asset, nonce, withdrawals);
}
The nonce parameter in these calls refers to a nonce that is keyed by the specific lane-asset pair,
which is then incremented after use:
function _batchWithdraw(
BridgeLane lane,
address asset,
uint64 suppliedNonce,
WithdrawalRecord[] calldata withdrawals
) private {
// [...]
uint64 expectedNonce = _withdrawNonce(bridgeStorage, lane, asset);
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 20 of 24
4.
Discussion

EVM Deposit/Withdraw Bridge Smart Contract Security Assessment
August 18, 2026
if (suppliedNonce != expectedNonce) revert UnexpectedWithdrawalNonce();
// [...]
_setWithdrawNonce(bridgeStorage, lane, asset, expectedNonce + 1);
// [...]
}
Because this nonce is only keyed by the specific lane-asset pair, it is not keyed by the withdrawer
address. This means that all of the accounts that do withdrawals on a given lane-asset pair have to
share the nonce parameter. One successful withdrawal by one withdrawer will affect the behavior
of all the other withdrawers because the nonce increased by one.
For a single EVM account, the EVM nonce field in the transaction enforces an order in which
pending transactions can be confirmed in a block. Once there are multiple EVM accounts,
however, this guarantee can no longer be enforced. The withdrawers have to coordinate among
themselves to ensure that each nonce is used only once, and, if any EVM block contains multiple
valid withdrawals, those withdrawals could be reordered by the block builder so that only one of
them succeeds.
Unlike an out-of-order EVM account nonce, an out-of-order nonce field on a transaction sent by a
different withdrawer is still eligible for inclusion in the block. The transaction would simply revert,
which means that the gas price is still paid and the EVM nonce is still consumed. The transaction
must then be re-signed and resubmitted later.
If these effects are undesirable, then we recommend reviewing the purpose of this nonce field and
updating the design accordingly. One alternative design would be to allow nonces to be
consumed in any order.
