This provides a description of the high-level components of the system and how they interact,
including details like a function’s externally controllable inputs and how an attacker could leverage
each input to cause harm or which invariants or constraints of the system are critical and must
always be upheld.
Not all components in the audit scope may have been modeled. The absence of a component in
this section does not necessarily suggest that it is safe.
5.1.
Component: PcfaBridge
Description
The PCFA bridge contract manages a portion of the customer funds entrusted to Pacifica on the
Monad EVM-compatible chain. It has a centralized owner, which, per documentation, must be a
multi-signature wallet. This owner manages the asset whitelist, the withdrawer roles, and the
pause/unpause guardian roles, and it can also upgrade the contract.
Attack surface
Any user can deposit assets or native value to the contract using the deposit and spotDeposit
functions, which deposit the asset using the Balance and Spot lanes, respectively.
Only the deposit and spotDeposit functions were reviewed as the formal attack-surface scope,
because they are the only functions that do not require a specifically assigned and trusted role.
The other functions were reviewed in the context of their higher-privilege callers.
Invariants
For the deposit and spotDeposit functions, the lane and asset parameters must match a
whitelisted combination, and the amount of the asset is actually transferred. The depositor can,
however, specify an arbitrary recipient address and arbitrary memo data. The net on-chain effect
of a deposit is the assignment of a new deposit nonce and the emission of a Deposit event.
The batchWithdraw and spotBatchWithdraw functions require the withdrawer role for the
corresponding lane-asset pair. Anyone holding that role can withdraw any amount of those assets
from the bridge. Withdrawal records must be specified, but they do not undergo further balance or
privilege checks. Each specific withdrawal either completes or emits a failure event.
In the event of a compromised, malfunctioning, or malicious asset implementation, the
withdrawer should still be able to submit withdrawals that attempt to move the impacted assets.
These withdrawals should not affect other withdrawals beyond the consumption of gas.
Conversely, in the event of a malfunctioning withdrawer, the withdrawer should not be able to
