CB-239,FR-386,Merkle verificationdelegated to the old relay accepts a zero leaf for unfinalized rounds,Zellic__Flare_Relay_Network_Diff_-_Zellic_Audit_Report.pdf,Zellic,flare,2026-08-25,no,yes,2026-09,Low,"Detailed Findings
2.1.
Merkle verificationdelegated to the old relay accepts a zero leaf for unfinalized
rounds
Target
Relay
Category
Coding Mistakes
Severity
Low
Likelihood
Low
Impact
Low
Description
This diff hardened the local branch of the verify() function in Relay so that a voting round without
a finalized root can no longer serve as a verification target. The stored root is read first and rejected
when it is zero:
bytes32 root = merkleRootsPrivate[_protocolId][_votingRoundId];
require(root != bytes32(0), NotFinalized());
require(_proof.verifyCalldata(root, _leaf), MerkleProofInvalid());
The guard is necessary because MerkleProof.processProofCalldata returns the leaf unchanged
when the proof is empty, which reduces verifyCalldata to an equality test between the leaf and
the root. A caller supplying a zero leaf and an empty proof therefore matches the zero root that every
unfinalized round holds.
However, the branch taken before it, which serves rounds below the migration boundary out of
the previously deployed relay, carries no equivalent guard. It forwards the caller's arguments un-
changed and only requires that the delegated verification call return true:
if (address(oldRelay) != address(0) && _votingRoundId <
startingVotingRoundIdForInitialRewardEpochId) {
// [...]
bool ok = oldRelay.verify(_protocolId, _votingRoundId, _leaf, _proof);
require(ok, OldRelayVerificationFailed());
// [...]
return true;
}
The legacy relay implementation does not enforce a finalization check of its own. It verifies proofs
directly against the stored root without checking that it is nonzero. Consequently, a caller can se-
lect an unfinalized round below the migration boundary, supply _leaf = bytes32(0) and an empty
_proof, and obtain true from verify().
