Page 9 of 26

Flare Relay Network Diff Smart Contract Patch Review
September 14, 2026
This means that the amount of native value sent with the call is decided at execution time, instead of
when the call is timelocked.
Impact
This relaxes the timelock guarantee a little. The amount of native value to send through the time-
locked call can change what the call does, especially if the call target branches on the amount of
native value it receives.
The current impact is minimal, because the only timelocked functions that take native value are ones
that use it on a fee. For these, the fee is either paid or unpaid, and so the timelock itself does mean-
ingfully commit the contract to a specific action.
Recommendations
There is no clean way to resolve this, because of two considerations. Currently, there is a UX con-
venience where governance calls that become timelocked can be issued by calling the function
normally, rather than having to encode the calldata and then call a general function. The second
consideration is that the contract should not, in general, store native value between transactions. If
both considerations are kept, then committing to an amount of native value for the duration of the
timelock would require storing it in a separate contract maintained by the FlareGovernance abstract
contract.
If additional payable governance timelocked functions are planned, then we recommend revisiting
this design choice to ensure that end users still benefit from the same degree of timelock guaran-
tees.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Flare, and a fix was implemented in commit cbd3b8e1 ↗. This
commit documents the behavior, clarifying that the choice of value escapes being timelocked.
