Description
The relay function parses isSecureRandom as a byte from the packed protocol message. For pro-
tocol IDs greater than 1, it does not reject values outside {0, 1}. The signed digest commits to the
raw message bytes, so a message containing a noncanonical value can be accepted if the required
signing quorum signs those exact bytes and the other relay checks pass.
The randomNumberProtocolId path normalizes the byte using iszero(iszero(...)) before using
it in the random-number proof, storage, and events. However, the other protocol-finalization path
copies the masked byte directly into event data:
mstore(add(memPtrFor, M_5_isSecureRandom),
structValue(
mload(memPtrFor),
MSG_NMR_BOFF_isSecureRandom,
MSG_NMR_MASK_isSecureRandom
)
)
mstore(add(memPtrFor, M_3), ""ProtocolMessageRelayed(uint8,uin"")
mstore(add(memPtrFor, M_4), ""t32,bool,bytes32)"")
log3(
add(memPtrFor, M_5_isSecureRandom), 64, keccak256(add(memPtrFor, M_3), 49),
protocolId, votingRoundId
)
Themask MSG_NMR_MASK_isSecureRandomis 0xff,soitpreservesvaluessuchas 2. Although Proto-
colMessageRelayeddeclares isSecureRandomas bool,theassembly log3bypassesSolidity'sevent
encoder and emits the value unchanged. This violates the ABI encoding requirement for booleans,
which permits only 0 and 1.
Impact
If a quorum signs a message with an isSecureRandom byte above 1 for a protocol other than ran-
domNumberProtocolId, the relay can finalize the Merkle root while emitting a log that strict ABI de-
coders reject. An indexer relying on decoded ProtocolMessageRelayed events may therefore fail to
Zellic © 2026
