This workflow expects _relayMessage to encode a call to relay(), but, the caller can encode any
call there. It is also unpermissioned, so anyone can be the caller. The only guards here are that the
return data must be 35 bytes long, and a subsequence of that data matches _messageHash, which is
also completely controlled by the caller.
Because the sender is the contract address itself for the nested call, this means that any function on
this contract that happens to return 35 bytes can be made by anyone.
Impact
There is no current impact, because the only function that returns 35 bytes is the intended relay()
call with the protocol ID set to 1.
However, there is no guard against a future update to this contract adding such a function. Hav-
ing the contract call itself may expose privileges that unpermissioned callers lack, for instance, cur-
rently, OwnableWithTimelock has a self-call check in executeTimelockedCall which, if not for the
state.executing check, would be exploitable.
Recommendations
We recommend checking the first four bytes of _relayMessage to ensure that it is actually a call to
relay().
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Flare, and a fix was implemented in commit 4ff9c6e0 ↗.
