3.1.
Some Tempo fee-paying entry points lack a caller-selected spend cap
Target
TempoAltTokenBase-based OFTs, RemoteHopV201Tempo,
RemoteMintRedeemHopTempo
ID
ZELLIC-WWETDM
Category
Business Logic
Severity
Low
Likelihood
Medium
Impact
Low
Description
The affected Tempo paths resolve a TIP-20 fee token for the caller and pull it to acquire the
endpoint-native LayerZeroEndpointDollar fee, but they do not accept a caller-selected
maximum TIP-20 spend.
The affected entry points are fixed OFT send interfaces, direct RemoteHopV201Tempo.sendOFT
calls, and the legacy two-argument RemoteMintRedeemHopTempo.mintRedeem overload. The
fee-inclusive wrapper and four-argument mint/redeem overload already accept bounds.
For a nonwhitelisted fee token, execution obtains an exact-output StablecoinDEX quote, adds
bounded settlement headroom, pulls that amount, and refunds the unused balance. Quote and
swap are atomic, but execution is not bound to a preview obtained before transaction inclusion.
The legacy mint/redeem overload makes this explicit by using type(uint256).max as its
fee-token cap.
Impact
A public TIP-403–authorized maker can change the executable StablecoinDEX price before
transaction inclusion, causing the caller to pay more fee token than previewed if its balance and
allowance permit.
In the sampled frxUSD route, moving a peg-priced preview to the -2% tick increases input by
about 1 / 0.98 - 1 = 2.0408% of the endpoint fee. Unused headroom is refunded, insufficient
balance or allowance reverts atomically, and bridge principal is unaffected.
Recommendations
Add feeToken and maxFeeTokenAmount to each affected API and enforce the cap before retaining
funds. Entry points that also quote the LayerZero fee internally should accept a caller-selected
maxNativeFee or prior MessagingFee.
