3.5.
A block-scoped oracle cache can outlive feed, scale, and validity changes
Target
FrxUSDCustodianWithOracle
ID
ZELLIC-NTTVDX
Category
Business Logic
Severity
Low
Likelihood
Low
Impact
Low
Description
Each state-changing conversion stores one raw oracle answer for the remainder of the block.
Every later conversion in that block returns the stored value without checking whether the
configured feed, answer, round, decimal scale, or freshness policy changed:
if (lastOracleUpdate == block.number) {
return lastSavedOraclePrice;
}
This creates several same-block inconsistencies:
• The setCustodianOracle function changes the feed and oracleDecimals without
invalidating the cached raw answer. The old feed's answer can therefore be interpreted
using the new feed's decimal scale.
• An eligible caller can prime the cache with a zero-amount conversion before the
existing feed changes its answer. Later conversions in the same block continue to use
the old answer.
• Tightening maximumOracleDelay or replacing a stale feed does not revalidate an
answer already cached in that block.
A searcher can order a zero-amount cache prime before a feed or owner update and execute a
favorable conversion after that update in the same block.
Impact
After an otherwise valid feed, answer, or freshness-policy change, a permissionless searcher can
convert against stale or incorrectly scaled pricing. A different-scale migration can expose the
transferable reserve or remaining mint capacity; same-feed and delay changes can also produce
favorable stale conversions.
A fork reproduction primed the cache, installed an economically equivalent feed with a different
scale, and withdrew the full sampled reserve with 1 frxUSD. Separate proofs covered same-feed
repricing and delay tightening. The required owner-approved migration or same-block feed
update makes the trigger rare, but demonstrated loss can be reserve-scale.
[...excerpt ends]"
