The same applies when the pair quotes through getAmountOut(amountIn, tokenIn), which is
also called with the nominal amount.
When tokenIn is a fee-on-transfer token, or a rebasing token that delivers 1 to 2 wei less (such as
stETH), the pair receives less than amountIn. The output was priced for the larger amount, so the
pair's invariant check fails and swap() reverts.
The route's measure flag does not help. It only changes the swap's basis amount to the executor's
actual balance, and the executor-to-pair transfer inside the step is still taxed. In two other cases,
there is no executor balance to measure at all:
1. Direct-funded first swap. The router pulls the user's tokens straight into the first pair.
With the measure flag set, the executor balance is 0 and the step is skipped, so the
route reverts with FSE:IO.
