receiver override. The credited amount is the nominal output, but the next pair receives
less.
For rebasing tokens on the default funding path, the executor receives amountIn - 1 from the
router and then tries to forward amountIn, which fails with a balance revert. For 1-wei shortfalls,
the invariant check only fails when 1 wei of input is worth about 1 wei of output or more.
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 29 of 505

frxUSD Ecosystem Smart Contract Security Assessment
October 19, 2026
The contract documentation states that the measure flag is ""needed for fee-on-transfer tokens"",
which stepUniV2() cannot deliver.
Impact
Routes whose UniV2 step takes a fee-on-transfer token, or a short-delivering rebasing token, as
input always revert, regardless of the measure flag. No funds are lost, because the revert rolls
back the whole transaction. These tokens cannot be swapped through UniV2-style steps, contrary
to what the documentation implies. Users must use another route, for example a generic call step
to a router that supports fee-on-transfer tokens.
Recommendations
Price the input from what the pair actually received. In stepUniV2(), send the input first (unless
the pair is already funded), and then derive amountIn from the pair's tokenIn balance above its
stored reserves, and quote and call swap() with that amount. This also covers direct funding and
receiver overrides, because the amount is measured at the pair. For Fraxswap pairs, run
executeVirtualOrders first and subtract the TWAMM reserves, since the pair holds the input
token of pending long-term orders. Consider rejecting this mode on a Fraxswap pair without
executeVirtualOrders, which would overstate amountIn and revert.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Frax, and a fix was implemented in commit 488caac2 ↗.
