Description
The custodians inherit an owner-only recovery function that can transfer any ERC-20 token,
including the active custodianTkn, without reducing frxUSDMinted:
function recoverERC20(address token, uint256 amount) external onlyOwner {
SafeERC20.safeTransfer(IERC20(token), owner(), amount);
}
Normal redemption burns frxUSD and decreases frxUSDMinted before transferring backing.
Recovery bypasses both steps. Because maxRedeem is capped by the remaining token balance, full
recovery can leave liability unchanged while reducing redeemable capacity to zero.
A fork proof reproduced that state. A sampled live recovery was surplus-only because local
frxUSDMinted was zero at the time.
Impact
Recovering active backing can leave local liability without matching reserves. Partial depletion
makes holders compete for the remaining balance, and later deposits can fund an existing
shortfall.
No non-owner reserve-removal path was identified. This is an explicit trusted-custody and
solvency assumption, not an unprivileged exploit.
Recommendations
Separate stray-token rescue from backing withdrawal. If active backing must be immovable, reject
token == address(custodianTkn). If rebalancing is required, allow only surplus above a gross,
fee-independent liability floor:
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 31 of 505

frxUSD Ecosystem Smart Contract Security Assessment
October 19, 2026
uint256 required = _convertToAssets(frxUSDMinted, Math.Rounding.Ceil);
uint256 balance = custodianTkn.balanceOf(address(this));
uint256 surplus = balance > required ? balance - required :
0;
if (amount > surplus) revert InsufficientSurplus();
Do not use previewRedeem for the floor because mutable fees and caller exemptions affect it.
Oracle-priced custodians should use one fresh coherent oracle observation. Emit and monitor
reserve, liability, and withdrawal values.
Remediation
Frax acknowledged the recovery authority as intended behavior. They stated that its owner
multi-signature wallet must be able to manage backing without delay and that frxUSD backing
may be held outside the custodians. No code change is planned; unrestricted owner recovery
remains an explicit trust and solvency assumption.
