3.10.
Rate-limit configuration transitions can reprice historical usage
Target
RateLimiterModule, RateLimiterLib
ID
ZELLIC-PKHXR5
Category
Business Logic
Severity
Informational
Likelihood
N/A
Impact
Informational
Description
Per-endpoint ID (EID) updates checkpoint usage under the old effective configuration before
replacement. The default setter only validates and replaces the default, without checkpointing
EIDs that inherit it.
On the next state calculation, each inheriting EID applies the new limit and window to all time
elapsed since lastUpdated, including time that occurred under the old configuration.
For example, an EID that fully consumes a 100-token, 100-second bucket and waits 10 seconds
should carry 90 tokens of usage into a replacement configuration. Replacing the default with 1,000
tokens per 10 seconds instead clears the old usage and reports 1,000 available rather than the
checkpoint-preserving 910.
A related edge occurs when a disabled per-EID override stores zero limits and windows.
Reenabling checkpoints under that zero-valued configuration, old usage is preserved while
lastUpdated advances, so the disabled interval contributes no refill. Otherwise, inactive
placeholder fields therefore determine historical accrual.
Impact
An authorized transition can expose extra capacity, preserve excess usage, or unexpectedly delay
refill. A default change affects inheriting EIDs; a zero-valued disable/reenable affects the selected
route and direction.
Both cases require owner configuration and grant no capacity beyond the replacement policy.
This is a transition-accounting hazard, not an unprivileged rate-limit bypass.
Recommendations
Preserve old-slope accrual without an unbounded scan. One option is a hard-bounded set of
default-backed EIDs that is checkpointed before replacement.
Otherwise, retain bounded version history and require each stale EID to apply every intervening
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
