3.13.
Freezing the zero address blocks mint and burn operations
Target
FrxUSD, FrxUSDOFTUpgradeable
ID
ZELLIC-AYPT3H
Category
Business Logic
Severity
Informational
Likelihood
N/A
Impact
Informational
Description
The owner or a delegated freezer can freeze any address, including address(0). Transfer hooks
reject an update when either endpoint or the caller is frozen:
function freeze(address account) external {
if (!isFreezer(msg.sender) && msg.sender != owner()) revert NotFreezer();
_freeze(account);
}
if (isFrozen(to) || isFrozen(from) || isFrozen(msg.sender)) revert IsFrozen();
ERC-20 minting uses from == address(0), while burning uses to == address(0). Freezing the
zero address therefore blocks every non-owner mint and burn on the affected contract instance.
OFT outbound settlement burns and inbound settlement mints, so both bridge directions are also
blocked.
Impact
A compromised or mistaken delegated freezer can turn a per-account compliance role into a
system-wide issuance, redemption, and bridge-liveness control. Ordinary nonzero-to-nonzero
transfers continue, and no assets are transferred to the freezer.
The trigger is privileged and reversible; an available owner can call thaw(address(0)), and the
owner already has an explicit pause capability. This makes the issue an Informational role-design
and liveness concern.
Recommendations
Reject zero in the shared internal freeze primitive so both single and batch entry points inherit the
check:
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 41 of 505

frxUSD Ecosystem Smart Contract Security Assessment
October 19, 2026
if (account == address(0)) revert InvalidFreezeTarget();
If delegated freezers are intentionally allowed to halt all mint and burn operations, expose a
separately named action, role, and event instead of using the zero address as an implicit global
sentinel.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Frax, and a fix was implemented in commit 0eb8908d ↗.
