3.15.
A shared timestamp can discard fractional rate-limit recovery
Target
RateLimiterModule, RateLimiterLib
ID
ZELLIC-BXHRC7
Category
Business Logic
Severity
Informational
Likelihood
N/A
Impact
Informational
Description
Each EID stores outbound usage, inbound usage, and one shared lastUpdated timestamp. Every
successful nonzero consumption in either direction projects both usage values and then persists
the current timestamp.
state.outboundUsage = uint112(_decayUsage(..., elapsed));
state.inboundUsage = uint112(_decayUsage(..., elapsed));
state.lastUpdated = currentTimestamp;
The _decayUsage function floors each projection independently:
uint256 replenished = (limit * elapsed) / window;
Opposite-direction traffic can materialize usage before one full local base unit accrues, discard the
fractional numerator, and advance the shared timestamp. For example, with a one-unit outbound
limit over two seconds, one successful inbound delivery per second can keep saturated outbound
usage unchanged across multiple outbound windows.
Impact
Once both directions are enabled, a caller can trigger the effect only by completing real
opposite-direction traffic. Each timestamp advancement discards less than one local base unit.
With at least six local decimals, even one materialization per second loses less than one
micro-token per second, or about 31.6 tokens per year.
Complete stalling requires a dust-scale limit-to-window ratio. The proof of concept used a
one-unit limit and a two-second window, and no affected deployed configuration was identified.
This is a bounded precision/liveness discrepancy, not a material rate-limit bypass.
Zellic © 2026
←Back to Contents
Page 45 of 505

frxUSD Ecosystem Smart Contract Security Assessment
October 19, 2026
Recommendations
Use independent outbound and inbound accrual timestamps, or carry each direction's division
remainder across updates. Bind any remainder to the active limit/window version, and clear it on
an explicit reset or configuration change.
Measure hot-path storage costs before adopting remainder accounting. Independent timestamps
or a minimum configured recovery rate may be simpler.
Remediation
This issue has been acknowledged by Frax, and a fix was implemented in commit 5359fde1 ↗.
