Description
Note: This finding was discovered by Frax during remediation.
Both FraxswapRouterMultihopV2.swap() and swapWithPermit() pay out to params.recipient,
or to msg.sender when it is address(0) — _swap() forwards this address to
FraxswapExecutorV2.execute() without validating it:
address recipient = params.recipient == address(0) ?
msg.sender :
params.recipient;
// no check that the recipient is not the router or the executor
amountOut = executor.execute{ value: value }(
params.route,
params.amountIn,
params.tokenOut,
payable(recipient),
params.amountOutMinimum
);
The execute() function measures the executor's whole balance of tokenOut and transfers it to
recipient. If the recipient is the executor, the transfer goes from the executor to itself and the
output stays in the contract. If the recipient is the router, ERC-20 output is sent to a contract that
has no function to forward or rescue tokens.
The executor's design makes the stranded funds unrecoverable by the user. The executor
documents that anything left behind is ""finders keepers"". Because execute() measures the full
tokenOut balance, the next swapper whose route ends in the same token receives the stranded
amount as part of their own output.
