The purpose of this section is to document miscellaneous observations that we made during the
assessment. These discussion notes are not necessarily security related and do not convey that
we are suggesting a code change.
4.1.
Additional review observations
The review also identified an issue in legacy contracts bundled with a scoped repository, a
test-evidence limitation, and a deployment constraint for the reviewed upgrade implementations.
They are recorded here for completeness and do not affect the in-scope finding count.
Legacy owner sentinels can reopen initialization after ownership renunciation
Reference ID: ZELLIC-VQC7AH
The dev-staked-frax-erc4626 repository also bundles legacy src/contracts/frxUSD/FrxUSD.sol,
FrxUSD2.sol, and FrxUSD3.sol contracts outside the declared custodian paths. Their initializer
uses owner() == address(0) as its initialization sentinel. Calling renounceOwnership() restores
that value, allowing a later caller to initialize the proxy, become the owner, authorize itself as a
minter, and mint. The bundled FrxUSD2 contract rejects a zero new owner but retains the mutable
sentinel; FrxUSD3 inherits it.
A local proxy proof reproduced the takeover for the bundled FrxUSD2 and FrxUSD3 contracts. The
trigger requires legacy governance to renounce ownership, but the subsequent takeover is
permissionless. These contracts are outside the declared custodian scope, and the in-scope
frax-tokens FrxUSD upgrade implementation does not expose the legacy initializer.
Use an irreversible initialization flag, and reject a zero owner. Existing proxies need an atomic
migration that sets the flag before ownership can become zero; otherwise, prohibit or override
renounceOwnership.
Reviewed implementations are upgrade-only targets
